/* 生成的文件，不要手改：node tools/pd-build.mjs 从 tools/pd/ 生成（inputs e2f635410ac47727，公式由 katex@0.16.11 转成 MathML）。 */
window.MOLASTRIA_PD = {"index":{"title":"AI Protein Design","sub":"From Rosetta to RFdiffusion, ESM and general-purpose models: what the companies and labs have actually done","eyebrow":"MOLASTRIA · AI PROTEIN DESIGN","h1":"AI Protein Design","question":"When you read “AI designed a new protein”, how much of it was computed and how much actually happened in a test tube?","lead":"Protein design is an inverse problem: structure prediction says “give me a sequence and I will tell you the shape”, design says “give me a shape and a function and I will write a sequence”. How that problem is solved has changed three times in twenty years: first physics-based scoring plus search (Rosetta, and Top7 in 2003), then deep learning taking over both the judging (AlphaFold-class models became the referee for designs) and the writing (ProteinMPNN writes sequences, RFdiffusion generates backbones), and in the last two years general-purpose models pushing in as well — in 2025 OpenAI redesigned two reprogramming factors with a small specialised model, and in November 2026 it reported that GPT-6-class models can design binders without a specialised design model in the loop. This section lays the results out: every one carries a level of evidence (validated in the wet lab / computational only / a company statement only) and a note on how open its weights are. Each chapter comes with a demo computed on the spot, so that the arithmetic behind the numbers everybody quotes — hit rates, K_D, k_cat/K_M, recovery — is explicit. The last chapter is about biosecurity: the same toolkit has to be able to design medicines while leaving screening able to recognise what should not be synthesised.","scope":"<b>How to read the labels.</b> Every chapter and every key point carries a status: history, theorem (provable mathematically), established experimentally (supported by wet-lab work and independent repetition), approximation (holds under the stated conditions), frontier (still changing fast), disputed (publicly challenged or not reproduced) and open. Demos carry one of three labels: exact (checked against closed forms), toy model (a minimal model built to show one thing clearly) and numerical experiment (computed live the way the real method works; repeatable with other parameters). Each row of the results table additionally carries a level of evidence and a note on how open its weights are. <b>This section covers published results and the concepts behind them and contains no laboratory protocols</b>; it does not deal with toxins, pathogens or any harmful protein, and the last chapter describes only how screening and governance work, never how to get around them.","levels":{"history":["History","Documented history and facts about the literature"],"proved":["Theorem","Provable mathematically, independent of any particular system"],"tested":["Established experimentally","Supported by wet-lab work, and not only in one laboratory"],"approx":["Approximation","Holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them"],"frontier":["Frontier","Still changing fast in 2025–2026; conclusions may age quickly"],"disputed":["Disputed","Publicly challenged, or not independently reproduced"],"open":["Open","Not yet achieved or explained by anyone"]},"demoKinds":{"exact":["Exact","Computed live and checked against closed forms (assumptions are listed under each demo)"],"toy":["Toy model","A minimal model built to show one thing clearly; not any real protein"],"experiment":["Numerical experiment","Computed live the way the real method works, then tested; repeatable with other parameters"]},"groups":[{"title":"The problem and its two older answers","ids":["why","fold","rosetta"]},{"title":"Deep learning takes over three jobs","ids":["plm","inverse","gen"]},{"title":"What has actually been made","ids":["binder","enzyme","general"]},{"title":"Telling real from claimed, and staying safe","ids":["wet","safe"]}],"chapters":[{"id":"why","n":"01","title":"This is an inverse problem","summary":"Prediction asks what shape a sequence folds into; design asks which sequence gives a chosen shape and function. The search space is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and the answer is not unique.","level":"history","demos":["seqspace"]},{"id":"fold","n":"02","title":"Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design","summary":"Folding is a funnel in physics and used to be an impossible search in computation. Since 2021 prediction has been good enough to referee designs — but a referee does not set the questions.","level":"tested","demos":["hp"]},{"id":"rosetta","n":"03","title":"Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved","summary":"Top7 in 2003 proved that folds absent from nature can be built; the designed enzymes of 2008 proved that activity is a different barrier. This generation’s methods and its failures are both worth reading, because deep learning inherited its workflow.","level":"history","demos":["bundle","stab"]},{"id":"plm","n":"04","title":"Structure is already in the sequences: coevolution and language models","summary":"Two positions that touch must change together. Turning that correlation into a direct coupling takes one matrix inversion; protein language models never form the matrix and absorb the same statistics into their parameters instead.","level":"tested","demos":["msa"]},{"id":"inverse","n":"05","title":"Inverse folding: writing a sequence for a backbone","summary":"ProteinMPNN raised recovery from Rosetta’s 32.9 % to 52.4 % and ran 200× faster, which gave the design pipeline a new heart. But “recovery” as a metric is misleading.","level":"tested","demos":["mpnn"]},{"id":"gen","n":"06","title":"Generating backbones: how a diffusion model grows a protein","summary":"Add noise to real structures step by step, then learn to run it backwards. RFdiffusion solved 23 of 25 motif-scaffolding benchmarks this way, with a 19 % experimental success rate for binders.","level":"tested","demos":["diff"]},{"id":"binder","n":"07","title":"Binders and antibodies: how to read a hit rate","summary":"Every group reported de novo binders between 2024 and 2026, with success rates from 9 % to 100 %. The numbers are not comparable, because the targets, the number of designs and the filtering all differ. This chapter lays them out.","level":"frontier","demos":["kd"]},{"id":"enzyme","n":"08","title":"Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range","summary":"Designing enzymes is a question of energy scale: catalysis needs the transition state stabilised by 1–2 kcal/mol more, and scoring functions cannot deliver that. The 2025–2026 breakthroughs came from better generators plus much harsher filtering.","level":"frontier","demos":["kcat"]},{"id":"general","n":"09","title":"General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is","summary":"The 2025 RetroSOX/RetroKLF results are a company post with no paper and no sequences; the GPT-6 binders of November 2026 have wet-lab numbers, but on a self-hosted PDF, and the filtering still relied on specialised models.","level":"frontier","demos":["screen"]},{"id":"wet","n":"10","title":"How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars","summary":"In the same field and the same year, “11.6 % actually bound” and “approaching solved” are both 2026 statements. The disagreement is usually not about attitude but about denominators, filtering and error bars.","level":"tested","demos":["evo"]},{"id":"safe","n":"11","title":"Biosecurity: keeping screening in step with the models","summary":"The same toolkit has to design medicines while leaving synthesis screening able to recognise what should not be made. This chapter is about how gates are layered, how disclosure works and which levers policy uses — and about no method for getting around any of them.","level":"frontier","demos":["layers"]}],"milestones":[{"year":"1935","text":"Eyring’s transition-state theory: rate is exponential in barrier height, the source of every conversion in chapter 8 (<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1973","text":"Anfinsen: sequence determines structure, so writing a sequence is in principle the same as specifying a structure (<a href=\"#c-why\" data-open=\"why\">01 · This is an inverse problem</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-anfinsen1973\" data-ref=\"anfinsen1973\">Anfinsen 1973</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1984","text":"The hydrophobic moment: periodicity on a helix becomes computable (<a href=\"#c-rosetta\" data-open=\"rosetta\">03 · Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eisenberg1984\" data-ref=\"eisenberg1984\">Eisenberg 1984</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1989","text":"Lau and Dill’s HP lattice model and Kauffman’s NK fitness landscape: two toy models still in use (<a href=\"#c-fold\" data-open=\"fold\">02 · Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design</a>, <a href=\"#c-wet\" data-open=\"wet\">10 · How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lau1989\" data-ref=\"lau1989\">Lau &amp; Dill 1989</a>; <a href=\"#ref-kauffman1989\" data-ref=\"kauffman1989\">Kauffman &amp; Weinberger 1989</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1996","text":"Designability: a few conformations are folded into by very many sequences, which may be why so few folds occur in nature (<a href=\"#c-fold\" data-open=\"fold\">02 · Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-li1996\" data-ref=\"li1996\">Li 1996</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2003","text":"Top7: the first entirely new fold at atomic accuracy, 1.17 Å from the design in the crystal (<a href=\"#c-rosetta\" data-open=\"rosetta\">03 · Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kuhlman2003\" data-ref=\"kuhlman2003\">Kuhlman 2003</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2008","text":"Kemp eliminases and retro-aldolases: the first de novo enzymes with measurable activity, and the first appearance of the “far too slow” problem that took twenty years to move (<a href=\"#c-rosetta\" data-open=\"rosetta\">03 · Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rothlisberger2008\" data-ref=\"rothlisberger2008\">Röthlisberger 2008</a>; <a href=\"#ref-jiang2008\" data-ref=\"jiang2008\">Jiang 2008</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2011","text":"Coevolution: contact maps and three-dimensional structures computed straight from the statistics of homologous sequences (<a href=\"#c-plm\" data-open=\"plm\">04 · Structure is already in the sequences: coevolution and language models</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marks2011\" data-ref=\"marks2011\">Marks 2011</a>; <a href=\"#ref-morcos2011\" data-ref=\"morcos2011\">Morcos 2011</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2017","text":"Tens of thousands of designs assayed at once; design becomes a statistical experiment (<a href=\"#c-rosetta\" data-open=\"rosetta\">03 · Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rocklin2017\" data-ref=\"rocklin2017\">Rocklin 2017</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2021","text":"AlphaFold2 and RoseTTAFold: structure prediction becomes good enough to referee designs (<a href=\"#c-fold\" data-open=\"fold\">02 · Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jumper2021\" data-ref=\"jumper2021\">Jumper 2021</a>; <a href=\"#ref-baek2021\" data-ref=\"baek2021\">Baek 2021</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2022","text":"ProteinMPNN: 52.4 % recovery, 200× faster, and the design pipeline gets a new heart (<a href=\"#c-inverse\" data-open=\"inverse\">05 · Inverse folding: writing a sequence for a backbone</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2023","text":"RFdiffusion and Chroma: diffusion models generate backbones directly, with a 19 % experimental success rate for binders (<a href=\"#c-gen\" data-open=\"gen\">06 · Generating backbones: how a diffusion model grows a protein</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a>; <a href=\"#ref-ingraham2023\" data-ref=\"ingraham2023\">Ingraham 2023</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2024","text":"AlphaFold 3 brings in nucleic acids and ligands; AlphaProteo reports binder success rates of 9–88 % on seven targets but releases no code (<a href=\"#c-fold\" data-open=\"fold\">02 · Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design</a>, <a href=\"#c-binder\" data-open=\"binder\">07 · Binders and antibodies: how to read a hit rate</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-abramson2024\" data-ref=\"abramson2024\">Abramson 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025 · 1","text":"ESM3’s esmGFP: only 58 % identical to its closest natural relative, and it really does glow (<a href=\"#c-plm\" data-open=\"plm\">04 · Structure is already in the sequences: coevolution and language models</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hayes2025\" data-ref=\"hayes2025\">Hayes 2025</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025 · 2","text":"De novo serine hydrolases reach k_cat/K_M = 2.2 × 10⁵ M⁻¹s⁻¹, about 60,000× earlier designed enzymes (<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lauko2025\" data-ref=\"lauko2025\">Lauko 2025</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025 · 3","text":"BindCraft is released open source with 10–100 % experimental success; Chai-2 designs antibodies zero-shot against 52 novel targets at a 15.5 % average hit rate (<a href=\"#c-binder\" data-open=\"binder\">07 · Binders and antibodies: how to read a hit rate</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pacesa2025\" data-ref=\"pacesa2025\">Pacesa 2025</a>; <a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025 · 4","text":"OpenCRISPR-1: a generated genome editor with about 95 % fewer off-target edits than SpCas9, its sequence released free of charge (<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ruffolo2025\" data-ref=\"ruffolo2025\">Ruffolo 2025</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025 · 5","text":"Biosecurity: assess, patch, then publish — coordinated disclosure applied to nucleic-acid synthesis screening for the first time (<a href=\"#c-safe\" data-open=\"safe\">11 · Biosecurity: keeping screening in step with the models</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wittmann2025\" data-ref=\"wittmann2025\">Wittmann 2025</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2025 · 6","text":"OpenAI and Retro Biosciences announce reprogramming factors redesigned by GPT-4b micro; there is still no paper, only the company’s own numbers (<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2025retro\" data-ref=\"openai2025retro\">OpenAI 2025 (company post)</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2026 · 1","text":"RFdiffusion2 takes active sites atom by atom and passes 41 of 41 enzyme-scaffolding benchmarks; 36 % of 135 designed metalloproteases cut the intended bond (<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ahern2026\" data-ref=\"ahern2026\">Ahern 2026</a>; <a href=\"#ref-chen2026\" data-ref=\"chen2026\">Chen 2026</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2026 · 2","text":"ESMFold2: antibody–antigen pass rates from sequence alone beat AlphaFold 3 with an alignment, and wet-lab minibinder hit rates reach 36–88 % (<a href=\"#c-plm\" data-open=\"plm\">04 · Structure is already in the sequences: coevolution and language models</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-candido2026\" data-ref=\"candido2026\">Candido 2026</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2026 · 3","text":"Evo 2 appears in Nature with its weights and nine-trillion-base dataset released; Pinal makes it possible to order a protein in a sentence (<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brixi2026\" data-ref=\"brixi2026\">Brixi 2026</a>; <a href=\"#ref-dai2026\" data-ref=\"dai2026\">Dai 2026</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2026 · 4","text":"AlphaProtein Novo from DeepMind and the Arnold lab: designed enzymes achieve selectivity no natural enzyme offers (<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wu2026\" data-ref=\"wu2026\">Wu 2026</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2026 · 5","text":"On 24 November 2026 OpenAI reports that GPT-6-class models design binders without a specialised design model: 35 of 322 bound, while conceding that filtering still relied on Boltz-2, OpenDDE and ESMFold2, so the pipeline end to end is not yet zero-shot (<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>]</span>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How many possible sequences does a 100-residue chain have?","opts":["About <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>30</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>130</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, fifty orders of magnitude more than the atoms in the observable universe","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>20</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","Proportional to the chain length"],"a":1,"why":"Twenty choices per position gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>100</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. That number is why design has to either restrict itself to a ball or learn a distribution and sample from it.","ch":"why"},{"q":"In the HP lattice model, what makes design harder than folding?","opts":["Folding has to enumerate sequences while design only enumerates conformations","Finding the ground state of a given sequence is one search, while most sequences have no unique ground state at all","They are equally hard","Design requires quantum chemistry"],"a":1,"why":"The demo runs all 4,096 sequences of length 12 and only a few per cent have a unique ground state, while a handful of conformations are each folded into by dozens of sequences — that is designability.","ch":"fold"},{"q":"Why does reading off “which two columns vary together” get contacts wrong?","opts":["Because alignments contain gaps","Because correlation is transitive: if i touches j and j touches k, then i and k correlate too","Because there are too few sequences","Because there are only 20 amino acids"],"a":1,"why":"The exact precision matrix (the inverse covariance) is identically zero at non-contacts while the correlation matrix is not; in the demo the two differ by a dozen orders of magnitude. That is why DCA inverts.","ch":"plm"},{"q":"Does a high “sequence recovery” mean an inverse-folding design will work?","opts":["Yes, recovery is the success rate","No: the native sequence is not the only answer, and high recovery only says the model agrees with nature’s choice","Recovery is inversely proportional to success","It only means anything above 90 %"],"a":1,"why":"At low temperature recovery is high but the sampled sequences are nearly identical (zero diversity), and at high temperature the reverse. What counts is the fraction that expresses, folds and functions in the wet lab.","ch":"inverse"},{"q":"In the reverse process of a diffusion model, what happens if you use only the posterior mean at each step instead of sampling?","opts":["The same result, only faster","The variance of the samples is systematically squeezed, so what comes out is over-smooth and biased towards the average","What comes out is more diverse","It violates conservation of probability"],"a":1,"why":"With a Gaussian prior this can be computed exactly: sampling the posterior each step gives a sample covariance equal to the prior (the demo shows a ratio of about 0.97), while the mean-only route keeps about 0.4.","ch":"gen"},{"q":"How much binding free energy does a hundredfold gain in affinity cost at room temperature?","opts":["About 0.3 kcal/mol","About 2.7 kcal/mol (1.36 per order of magnitude)","About 27 kcal/mol","It does not depend on temperature"],"a":1,"why":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and at 298.15 K, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.3642</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol, so two orders of magnitude is 2.73 kcal/mol — the size of one or two hydrogen bonds.","ch":"binder"},{"q":"A de novo enzyme is four orders of magnitude slower than a natural one. How much activation free energy is that?","opts":["About 0.5 kcal/mol","About 5.5 kcal/mol","About 55 kcal/mol","It cannot be converted"],"a":1,"why":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.3642</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5.46</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol. The gap sounds huge but in energy it is barely more than one hydrogen bond — which is the hope, and also why a 2 kcal/mol scoring error ruins everything.","ch":"enzyme"},{"q":"With a 1 % prior hit rate, a filter at AUC 0.92 and only the top 7 % of scores kept, what hit rate do you see at the bench?","opts":["Still 1 %","About 9 %, an enrichment of about ninefold","About 92 %","100 %"],"a":1,"why":"The hit rate is prior·sensitivity / (prior·sensitivity + (1 − prior)·false-positive rate) — Bayes’ rule. Improving the filter raises the hit rate more than improving the generator, and the filter is what actually got stronger in several 2026 reports.","ch":"wet"},{"q":"Of 3,766 published binder designs, what fraction actually bound in experiments?","opts":["About 70 %","11.6 %","About 50 %","Nobody has counted"],"a":1,"why":"The 2025 meta-analysis also found that the same filter metric has a precision anywhere from 0.1 to 1.0 depending on the target — so “our hit rate is X %” means nothing without naming the target.","ch":"wet"},{"q":"What is the level of evidence for the RetroSOX / RetroKLF results OpenAI announced in 2025?","opts":["A peer-reviewed paper","A company post only: no paper, no preprint and no released sequences, so it cannot be checked independently","A preprint awaiting publication","Independently reproduced by a third party"],"a":1,"why":"As of November 2026 a full-text search of OpenAlex finds no scholarly record of RetroSOX or RetroKLF, and the company page links no manuscript. The numbers may well be right, but for now they can only be read as a company statement.","ch":"general"},{"q":"In OpenAI’s GPT-6 protein-design post of November 2026, what limitation does it concede itself?","opts":["The hit rate is too low","Filtering still relied on specialised models (Boltz-2, OpenDDE, ESMFold2), so the pipeline end to end is not yet zero-shot; and predicting a whole structure remains a clear weakness (TM-score 0.26–0.38 against ESMFold2’s 0.93)","There was no wet-lab work","Only one target was tested"],"a":1,"why":"The limitations section says so explicitly, and also warns that “unusually high accuracy may partly reflect memorization or patterns left by the process used to generate those structures”. That kind of self-limitation is worth reading in the original.","ch":"general"},{"q":"Three independent screening gates each have 90 % sensitivity. What is the overall miss rate? And if they all lean on the same database?","opts":["0.1 % / 10 %","10 % / 10 %","30 % / 90 %","0.1 % / 0.1 %"],"a":0,"why":"Independent gates multiply: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.</mn><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Under full correlation the overall sensitivity collapses to the best single gate, so the miss rate is 10 %. That hundredfold difference is the whole argument for making the gates independent of each other.","ch":"safe"}],"books":[{"title":"Protein Actions: Principles and Modeling","authors":"Ivet Bahar, Robert L. Jernigan, Ken A. Dill (Garland Science, 2017)","note":"The physics of folding, stability and binding; both the HP model and the elastic network model come from this lineage."},{"title":"Molecular Driving Forces","authors":"Ken A. Dill, Sarina Bromberg (Garland Science, 2nd ed. 2010)","note":"An introduction to statistical thermodynamics covering the free-energy arithmetic of the demos in chapters 7 and 12."},{"title":"Structural Bioinformatics","authors":"Jenny Gu, Philip E. Bourne (eds., Wiley-Blackwell, 2nd ed. 2009)","note":"Background on structural data, alignment and scoring functions; useful before reading the CASP assessment papers."},{"title":"Deep Learning","authors":"Ian Goodfellow, Yoshua Bengio, Aaron Courville (MIT Press, 2016)","note":"The foundations of the networks and generative models used in chapters 5 and 6."},{"title":"Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning","authors":"Christopher M. Bishop (Springer, 2006)","note":"Full derivations of the graphical models and precision matrices of chapter 4 and the Bayesian decisions of chapter 9."},{"title":"Directed Enzyme Evolution: Advances and Applications","authors":"Miguel Alcalde (ed., Springer, 2017)","note":"The experimental half of chapter 10: how libraries are built and screened, and why epistasis makes gains sub-additive."}],"open":[{"q":"Polar targets are still hard. Hydrophobic interfaces design well; interfaces full of charges and hydrogen bonds (cytokines, sugars, disordered regions) have markedly lower success rates, and the Baker lab’s own 2026 review lists this as the first unsolved problem.","ch":"binder"},{"q":"Designed enzymes remain orders of magnitude below natural ones and succeed mainly on reactions with low barriers. Carbon–carbon bond formation and C–H activation have no de novo success story yet.","ch":"enzyme"},{"q":"Why does the same method succeed 80 % of the time on one target and never on another? TNFα has defeated several groups independently (AlphaProteo, PXDesign and others) and there is still no agreed explanation.","ch":"binder"},{"q":"Why is the correlation between computed scores and experimental binding so target-dependent? In the 3,766-design meta-analysis the same metric’s precision ranges from 0.1 to 1.0, and adding features does not help on unseen targets.","ch":"wet"},{"q":"Benchmarks and homology leakage. How much do “zero-shot” variant-effect scores inflate when homologues sit in the training set? Several 2025–2026 papers are redoing these benchmarks and the answer is not settled.","ch":"wet"},{"q":"Where is the line between general and specialised models? OpenAI concedes its pipeline still needs specialised structure models. Whether a general model can go the whole way is the question to watch in 2027.","ch":"general"},{"q":"Why do designed proteins come out “too stable”? Melting temperatures above 90 °C are routine, yet natural proteins need flexibility, degradability and regulation. There is no method yet for designing a protein that is deliberately only just stable enough.","ch":"rosetta"},{"q":"Immunogenicity. Will a designed protein be seen as foreign in a human body? Methods for removing T-cell epitopes by design are early, and this is one of the main obstacles to clinical translation.","ch":"binder"},{"q":"Can layered screening keep up with the models? The 2025 assessment is a success story for patch-before-publish, but the same cycle has to be repeated for every new generation of models, and screening capacity is very unevenly distributed.","ch":"safe"}],"bridge":{"intro":"Protein design can look far from chemistry — no balanced equations, no yields, no NMR spectra — yet every step is a move chemists already know: write a scoring function, use a cheaper model to throw away most candidates, validate the survivors experimentally, compute a free-energy difference. In each row below, the left is a concept from this section, the middle is its counterpart in chemistry research, and the right says which chapter and where else on this site to read on.","head":["In AI protein design","Its counterpart in chemistry research","Chapter","Read on"],"rows":[{"ai":"Sequence space <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","chem":"Chemical space: there are far more possible molecules than can be enumerated, so you generate and filter","ch":"why","go":"<a href=\"../chem-ml/#c-gen\">Chemical machine learning: generative models</a>"},{"ai":"Structure prediction as referee","chem":"Scoring candidates with density-functional theory or a machine-learning potential before deciding what to make","ch":"fold","go":"<a href=\"../dft/\">Density-functional theory</a>"},{"ai":"A physics-based score (the Rosetta energy)","chem":"A force field: bond, angle, van der Waals, electrostatic and solvation terms added up","ch":"rosetta","go":"<a href=\"../md/#c-ff\">Molecular dynamics: force fields</a>"},{"ai":"The hydrophobic moment and the heptad repeat","chem":"Amphiphilicity and self-assembly: the same periodicity decides how surfactants aggregate","ch":"rosetta","go":"<a href=\"../chemistry/physical/\">Physical chemistry hall</a>"},{"ai":"Coevolution and direct coupling analysis","chem":"Inverting an observed correlation matrix to remove indirect correlations (graphical models, partial correlation)","ch":"plm","go":"<a href=\"../fundamental-science/math/\">Mathematical foundations: probability and statistics</a>"},{"ai":"Protein language models","chem":"Chemical language models trained on SMILES strings or reaction equations","ch":"plm","go":"<a href=\"../chem-ml/#c-repr\">Chemical machine learning: molecular representation</a>"},{"ai":"Inverse folding (write a sequence for a backbone)","chem":"Retrosynthesis: find a route to a product — the same one-to-many inverse problem","ch":"inverse","go":"<a href=\"../code/\">Code Desk</a>"},{"ai":"Diffusion models that generate backbones","chem":"Diffusion models that generate crystal structures and conformers (the MatterGen family)","ch":"gen","go":"<a href=\"../world-models/#c-molecular\">World models: molecules and materials</a>"},{"ai":"Equivariance (rotate the structure and the prediction rotates with it)","chem":"The symmetry a machine-learning potential must obey, and irreducible representations in group theory","ch":"gen","go":"<a href=\"../nequip/\">NequIP</a> · <a href=\"../group-theory/\">Group theory</a>"},{"ai":"Converting between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","chem":"Binding free energy: 1.36 kcal/mol per order of magnitude, the same arithmetic as ligand binding or adsorption","ch":"binder","go":"<a href=\"../md/#c-fep\">Molecular dynamics: alchemical free energies</a>"},{"ai":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and transition-state stabilisation","chem":"Transition-state theory: every 1.36 kcal/mol of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is a tenfold rate change","ch":"enzyme","go":"<a href=\"../reaction/\">Reaction kinetics and transition states</a>"},{"ai":"A general-purpose model writing sequences directly","chem":"Using a general model for synthesis planning and experiment design, with specialised models to check it","ch":"general","go":"<a href=\"../ai-math/\">Frontier AI mathematics</a>"},{"ai":"Hit rates and the Bayes of filters","chem":"The enrichment factor of virtual screening: prior, resolving power and threshold set the hit rate","ch":"wet","go":"<a href=\"../chem-ml/#c-eval\">Chemical machine learning: traps of evaluation</a>"},{"ai":"Directed evolution versus model-guided design","chem":"Design of experiments: with the same budget, climb, randomise, or let a model choose","ch":"wet","go":"<a href=\"../chem-ml/#c-bo\">Chemical machine learning: Bayesian optimisation</a>"},{"ai":"Synthesis screening and layered defence","chem":"Controlled-substance and precursor lists, and laboratory safety review","ch":"safe","go":"<a href=\"../chemistry/chemeng/\">Chemical engineering hall</a>"}]},"families":{"pred":["Structure prediction","Structure or complex from sequence; used as the referee in a design pipeline"],"plm":["Sequence models","Language models trained on sequences, or on genomes"],"inverse":["Inverse folding","Write a sequence for a given backbone"],"gen":["Backbone generation","Generate new three-dimensional backbones or all-atom structures"],"binder":["Binders and antibodies","Design a protein that binds a chosen target"],"enzyme":["Enzymes","Design an active site that catalyses a reaction"],"platform":["Platforms and products","Large-scale experiments, released sequences, things that reached the clinic"],"eval":["Evaluation and benchmarks","Checking in hindsight whether the methods really work"],"safety":["Biosecurity and governance","Screening, disclosure and policy"]},"catalog":[{"name":"AlphaFold2","year":2021,"org":"Google DeepMind","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"A median backbone error of 0.96 Å at CASP14 against 2.8 Å for the next best method, which made single-chain structure prediction a usable tool","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-jumper2021\" data-ref=\"jumper2021\">Jumper 2021</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"RoseTTAFold","year":2021,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"A three-track network, later rebuilt into the backbone of RFdiffusion","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-baek2021\" data-ref=\"baek2021\">Baek 2021</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"AlphaFold-Multimer","year":2021,"org":"Google DeepMind","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"A 70 % heteromeric interface success rate (DockQ ≥ 0.23) on 4,446 complexes; it remains a preprint and was never published","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-evans2021\" data-ref=\"evans2021\">Evans 2021</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"AlphaFold3","year":2024,"org":"Google DeepMind","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"Proteins, nucleic acids, ligands and ions predicted jointly in one diffusion model; the code was opened at the end of 2024 but the weights need an application and are non-commercial only","exp":"silico","weights":"gated","src":"<a href=\"#ref-abramson2024\" data-ref=\"abramson2024\">Abramson 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Boltz-1 / Boltz-2","year":2025,"org":"MIT","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"An openly licensed (MIT) reimplementation at AlphaFold 3 level; Boltz-2 added affinity prediction and became the filter of choice in many design pipelines","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-wohlwend2024\" data-ref=\"wohlwend2024\">Wohlwend 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-passaro2025\" data-ref=\"passaro2025\">Passaro 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Chai-1","year":2024,"org":"Chai Discovery","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"Another complex-prediction model of the same class, open for non-commercial use","exp":"silico","weights":"research","src":"<a href=\"#ref-boitreaud2024\" data-ref=\"boitreaud2024\">Chai-1 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"HelixFold3","year":2024,"org":"Baidu PaddleHelix","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"Ligand poses close to AlphaFold 3 and antigen–antibody success above 80 % when five epitope residues are given, but still behind AlphaFold 3 on protein–protein","exp":"silico","weights":"research","src":"<a href=\"#ref-helixfold2024\" data-ref=\"helixfold2024\">HelixFold3 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Protenix-v2","year":2026,"org":"ByteDance Seed","fam":"pred","ch":"fold","learns":"Code and weights both Apache-2.0, commercial use included; 9–13 percentage points more success at DockQ &gt; 0.23 than v1","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-zhang2026\" data-ref=\"zhang2026\">Protenix-v2 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ESM-1b","year":2021,"org":"Meta FAIR","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"Structure and function information emerging from unsupervised training on 250 million sequences","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-rives2021\" data-ref=\"rives2021\">Rives 2021</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ESM-2 / ESMFold","year":2023,"org":"Meta FAIR","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"Structure from a single sequence with no alignment, fast enough to fold hundreds of millions of metagenomic sequences","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-lin2023\" data-ref=\"lin2023\">Lin 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ESM3 / esmGFP","year":2025,"org":"EvolutionaryScale","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"The generated fluorescent protein esmGFP is only 58 % identical to its closest natural relative (36 % to avGFP) and does fluoresce; the “500 million years of evolution” figure is an estimate fitted to six anthozoan pairs, not a measurement","exp":"wet","weights":"gated","src":"<a href=\"#ref-hayes2025\" data-ref=\"hayes2025\">Hayes 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ESMC / ESMFold2","year":2026,"org":"EvolutionaryScale, Biohub","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"A 50 ± 2 % antibody–antigen DockQ pass rate from sequence alone, above AlphaFold 3’s 47 ± 2 % with an alignment; wet-lab minibinder hit rates of 36–88 % and scFv 15–29 % on five targets, with affinities from 68 pM to 70 nM","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-candido2026\" data-ref=\"candido2026\">Candido 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ProGen2","year":2023,"org":"Profluent, Salesforce","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"An autoregressive language model that generates sequences conditioned on a family","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-nijkamp2023\" data-ref=\"nijkamp2023\">Nijkamp 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ProGen3","year":2025,"org":"Profluent","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"Eight models from 112 M to 46 B parameters: the share of generated sequences that are “valid” in an expression assay rises from 4 % (ProGen2-XL) to 49 % (46 B) — rare direct evidence that scale buys hit rate","exp":"wet","weights":"closed","src":"<a href=\"#ref-bhatnagar2025\" data-ref=\"bhatnagar2025\">Bhatnagar 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"xTrimoPGLM","year":2025,"org":"BioMap, Tsinghua University","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"A hundred-billion-parameter protein language model that both understands and generates","exp":"silico","weights":"research","src":"<a href=\"#ref-chen2025\" data-ref=\"chen2025\">Chen 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"SaProt","year":2023,"org":"Westlake University","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"Folding structure into part of the vocabulary and training it together with sequence","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-su2023\" data-ref=\"su2023\">Su 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Pro-PRIME","year":2024,"org":"SJTU, Shanghai AI Laboratory","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"Temperature-guided redesign of enzyme stability and activity with wet-lab data; an erratum followed in 2025","exp":"wet","weights":"research","src":"<a href=\"#ref-jiang2024\" data-ref=\"jiang2024\">Jiang 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"AlphaMissense","year":2023,"org":"Google DeepMind","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"Calls pathogenicity for 89 % of all 71 million possible missense variants","exp":"silico","weights":"closed","src":"<a href=\"#ref-cheng2023\" data-ref=\"cheng2023\">Cheng 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Evo","year":2024,"org":"Arc Institute","fam":"plm","ch":"plm","learns":"Trained on DNA, so one model covers genes, RNA and proteins at once","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-nguyen2024\" data-ref=\"nguyen2024\">Nguyen 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Evo 2","year":2026,"org":"Arc Institute, NVIDIA","fam":"plm","ch":"general","learns":"Nine trillion bases and a million-base context, zero-shot variant-effect prediction, with weights and dataset fully released; being a nucleotide model it produces no three-dimensional structure, so structure-conditioned design still needs another model","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-brixi2026\" data-ref=\"brixi2026\">Brixi 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ProteinMPNN","year":2022,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"inverse","ch":"inverse","learns":"52.4 % sequence recovery against Rosetta’s 32.9 % and 200× faster; redesigning ligand-binding scaffolds that Rosetta had failed on lifted AlphaFold self-consistency from 2.7 % to 54.1 %","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ESM-IF1","year":2022,"org":"Meta FAIR","fam":"inverse","ch":"inverse","learns":"Inverse folding trained on twelve million predicted structures, showing that predicted structures work as training data","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-hsu2022\" data-ref=\"hsu2022\">Hsu 2022</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"LigandMPNN","year":2025,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"inverse","ch":"inverse","learns":"Conditioning on the atoms of ligands, nucleic acids and metals: recovery near metal sites rises from 36–41 % to 77.5 %, and more than 100 designed interfaces bound experimentally","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2025\" data-ref=\"dauparas2025\">Dauparas 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Network hallucination","year":2021,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"gen","ch":"gen","learns":"Optimise the input sequence until the structure network is unusually confident about its own prediction, and a new structure falls out — the stopgap before generative models","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-anishchenko2021\" data-ref=\"anishchenko2021\">Anishchenko 2021</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"FrameDiff","year":2023,"org":"MIT","fam":"gen","ch":"gen","learns":"Treat the backbone as a chain of rigid-body frames and diffuse on SE(3)","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-yim2023\" data-ref=\"yim2023\">Yim 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"RFdiffusion","year":2023,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"gen","ch":"gen","learns":"Solves 23 of 25 motif-scaffolding benchmarks (hallucination 15, inpainting 19); 19 % of 95 designs per target on five targets bound experimentally, and p53-scaffolding binders reached 0.5 and 0.7 nM","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Chroma","year":2023,"org":"Generate Biomedicines","fam":"gen","ch":"gen","learns":"Programmable conditioning: symmetry, shape, secondary structure, even a sentence of text","exp":"wet","weights":"research","src":"<a href=\"#ref-ingraham2023\" data-ref=\"ingraham2023\">Ingraham 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Genie 2","year":2024,"org":"Columbia University","fam":"gen","ch":"gen","learns":"An academic, openly released backbone generator at the scale of the known structural universe","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-lin2024\" data-ref=\"lin2024\">Genie 2 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Proteina","year":2025,"org":"NVIDIA","fam":"gen","ch":"gen","learns":"Flow matching plus scale for backbone generation, with hierarchical fold-class conditioning","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-geffner2025\" data-ref=\"geffner2025\">Geffner 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"RFdiffusion2","year":2026,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"gen","ch":"enzyme","learns":"Active sites specified atom by atom with no inverse-rotamer sampling and no residue numbering: 41 of 41 enzyme-scaffolding benchmarks pass, against RFdiffusion’s 16","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-ahern2026\" data-ref=\"ahern2026\">Ahern 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Pinal","year":2026,"org":"Westlake University","fam":"gen","ch":"general","learns":"Order a protein in one sentence: 16 B parameters trained on 1.7 B protein–text pairs over 135 M proteins; in the wet lab 1 of 29 tested GFP designs (from 50,000 generated) was active, 1 of 94 for a PETase (from 200,000), 5 of 5 for an alcohol dehydrogenase and 5 of 20 for an H-protein","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-dai2026\" data-ref=\"dai2026\">Dai 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"AnewOmni","year":2026,"org":"Tsinghua University, ByteDance, Anew Therapeutics","fam":"gen","ch":"binder","learns":"One all-atom model for peptides, macrocycles, nanobodies and small molecules: for KRAS G12D, 7 of 30 linear peptides gave over 50 % inhibition (best IC₅₀ 2.4 µM), 7 of 20 macrocycles were below 100 µM and 3 of 4 nanobodies reached micromolar; for PCSK9 a 2.70 Å crystal structure sits 0.92 Å from the designed pose","exp":"wet","weights":"unclear","src":"<a href=\"#ref-kong2026\" data-ref=\"kong2026\">Kong 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"CheMoDesign","year":2026,"org":"Peking University","fam":"gen","ch":"binder","learns":"Design around non-canonical amino acids and post-translational modifications (sulfotyrosine, nitrotyrosine, photo-crosslinkers), with per-campaign counts reported: a carbonic anhydrase IX inhibitor at K_D 45.4 nM and an IL-7Rα binder at 8.4 nM","exp":"wet","weights":"unclear","src":"<a href=\"#ref-li2026\" data-ref=\"li2026\">Li 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"AlphaProteo","year":2024,"org":"Google DeepMind","fam":"binder","ch":"binder","learns":"Success rates of 9–88 % across seven targets with affinities down to 82 pM (IL-7Rα), and a complete failure on TNFα (0 of 54); no code or weights were released, with biosecurity given as the reason","exp":"wet","weights":"closed","src":"<a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"BindCraft","year":2025,"org":"EPFL","fam":"binder","ch":"binder","learns":"Gradient optimisation straight through AlphaFold2’s own weights, one shot per design; experimental success rates of 10–100 %, open source (MIT), and now everyone’s baseline","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-pacesa2025\" data-ref=\"pacesa2025\">Pacesa 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Chai-2","year":2025,"org":"Chai Discovery","fam":"binder","ch":"binder","learns":"Zero-shot antibody design on 52 novel targets: a 15.5 % average hit rate (20.0 % VHH, 13.7 % scFv) with at most 20 designs per target and at least one binder for 26 of 52 targets; weights not released","exp":"wet","weights":"closed","src":"<a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"PXDesign","year":2025,"org":"ByteDance Seed","fam":"binder","ch":"binder","learns":"Nanomolar hit rates of 17–82 % on six of seven targets (PD-L1 14 of 17) and 2–6× AlphaProteo on five of them; TNF-α still failed completely","exp":"wet","weights":"unclear","src":"<a href=\"#ref-pxdesign2025\" data-ref=\"pxdesign2025\">PXDesign 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"RFantibody","year":2025,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"binder","ch":"binder","learns":"De novo antibodies: the preprint screened about 9,000 designs per target by yeast display and the authors call the success rate “still quite low”; the best affinity was 78 nM against influenza HA, and of five complexes imaged by cryo-EM four matched the prediction","exp":"wet","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-bennett2025\" data-ref=\"bennett2025\">Bennett 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Atomistic binder design","year":2026,"org":"NVIDIA","fam":"binder","ch":"binder","learns":"Atomistic binder design with generative pretraining and test-time compute, following on from Proteina","exp":"silico","weights":"unclear","src":"<a href=\"#ref-didi2026\" data-ref=\"didi2026\">Didi 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"GPT-6 binders","year":2026,"org":"OpenAI, Onava","fam":"binder","ch":"general","learns":"35 of 322 minibinders (10.9 %) bound (PD-L1 5, IL-7Rα 3, TrkA 1, VEGF-A 8, IL-17A 18); rewriting sequences for existing backbones did far better (MDM2 75 of 88) and antibody-loop redesign 128 of 182; the post itself notes this is below the hit rates of the original RFdiffusion study and that Boltz-2, OpenDDE and ESMFold2 were used to filter candidates","exp":"selfpub","weights":"closed","src":"<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>; <a href=\"#ref-gpt6preprint\" data-ref=\"gpt6preprint\">GPT-6 preprint 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Kemp eliminase","year":2008,"org":"UW","fam":"enzyme","ch":"rosetta","learns":"Eight of 59 designs on 17 scaffolds had measurable activity with accelerations up to 10⁵, and directed evolution then improved one of them 200-fold","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-rothlisberger2008\" data-ref=\"rothlisberger2008\">Röthlisberger 2008</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Retro-aldolase","year":2008,"org":"UW","fam":"enzyme","ch":"rosetta","learns":"Four catalytic motifs with accelerations up to 10⁴, and crystal structures in two scaffolds matching the designs","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-jiang2008\" data-ref=\"jiang2008\">Jiang 2008</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"De novo serine hydrolases","year":2025,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"enzyme","ch":"enzyme","learns":"Hit rates climb with the filter from 1.6 % (2 of 129) to 73 % (8 of 11); the best, momi120-103, reaches k_cat/K_M = 2.2 × 10⁵ M⁻¹s⁻¹, about 60,000× earlier designed enzymes and inside the natural range","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-lauko2025\" data-ref=\"lauko2025\">Lauko 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"De novo metallohydrolases","year":2025,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"enzyme","ch":"enzyme","learns":"Five active designs of 96 in the first round and 11 in the second; ZETA_2 reaches k_cat/K_M = 53,000 M⁻¹s⁻¹ where earlier designed metalloenzymes managed 3–60, and its crystal structure sits 0.8 Å from the design","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-kim2025\" data-ref=\"kim2025\">Kim 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"De novo metalloproteases","year":2026,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"enzyme","ch":"enzyme","learns":"36 % of 135 designs cut the peptide at the intended bond (15 % for zinc-only active sites, 88 % for zinc-plus-water); the best accelerates hydrolysis more than 10⁸-fold and over 10¹⁰ after four substitutions, still 10³–10⁴ short of the most efficient natural proteases","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-chen2026\" data-ref=\"chen2026\">Chen 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"AlphaProtein Novo","year":2026,"org":"Google DeepMind, Caltech","fam":"enzyme","ch":"enzyme","learns":"Kemp eliminase hit rates from 3 % to 34 % over 1,536 novel-scaffold designs and esterases from 0 % to 44 % over 1,569, the best reaching k_cat/K_M = 3.6 × 10⁵ M⁻¹s⁻¹; a designed nitrene transferase achieved 99:1 six-ring selectivity and 94 % enantiomeric excess, which no natural enzyme manages","exp":"wet","weights":"gated","src":"<a href=\"#ref-wu2026\" data-ref=\"wu2026\">Wu 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Top7","year":2003,"org":"UW","fam":"platform","ch":"rosetta","learns":"A 93-residue fold with no natural counterpart, 1.17 Å from the design in the crystal, with a circular-dichroism spectrum at 98 °C almost identical to the one at 25 °C","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-kuhlman2003\" data-ref=\"kuhlman2003\">Kuhlman 2003</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Massively parallel stability assay","year":2017,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"platform","ch":"rosetta","learns":"Tens of thousands of designs assayed for stability at once, turning design into a statistical experiment and measuring for the first time how accurate the scoring functions really are","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-rocklin2017\" data-ref=\"rocklin2017\">Rocklin 2017</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"SKYCovione (GBP510)","year":2023,"org":"UW IPD, SK bioscience","fam":"platform","ch":"binder","learns":"A vaccine built on a designed icosahedral nanoparticle: in interim phase 3 results from 4,036 participants the neutralising titre was 2.93× the comparator, and it was approved in South Korea in 2022 — the first designed protein to become an approved medicine","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-gbp510\" data-ref=\"gbp510\">GBP510 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Mega-scale stability dataset","year":2023,"org":"UW, Rocklin lab","fam":"platform","ch":"wet","learns":"Folding free energies measured for hundreds of thousands of sequences, now both the training set and the touchstone for stability predictors","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-tsuboyama2023\" data-ref=\"tsuboyama2023\">Tsuboyama 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"OpenCRISPR-1","year":2025,"org":"Profluent","fam":"platform","ch":"general","learns":"Picked from four million generated CRISPR-Cas sequences with 209 proteins assayed in human cells: OpenCRISPR-1 differs from SpCas9 at 403 amino acids (71.7 % identity), with median editing of 55.7 % against 48.3 % and median off-target 0.32 % against 6.1 %; the model is closed but the protein sequence was released free of charge","exp":"wet","weights":"seq","src":"<a href=\"#ref-ruffolo2025\" data-ref=\"ruffolo2025\">Ruffolo 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"GPT-4b micro (RetroSOX / RetroKLF)","year":2025,"org":"OpenAI, Retro Biosciences","fam":"platform","ch":"general","learns":"A scaled-down GPT-4o specialised for protein engineering, used to redesign SOX2 (317 residues) and KLF4 (513): the company post reports a more than 50-fold increase in reprogramming-marker expression, variants differing from wild type by over 100 amino acids on average, and a hit rate of “nearly 50 %” for RetroKLF. There is no paper, no preprint and no released sequence, and a full-text search of OpenAlex finds nothing, so none of it can be checked independently","exp":"claim","weights":"closed","src":"<a href=\"#ref-openai2025retro\" data-ref=\"openai2025retro\">OpenAI 2025 (company post)</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"ProteinGym","year":2023,"org":"Harvard Medical School","fam":"eval","ch":"wet","learns":"The standard benchmark for variant-effect prediction, and the battleground of the homology-leakage debate","exp":"silico","weights":"open","src":"<a href=\"#ref-notin2023\" data-ref=\"notin2023\">Notin 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"CASP15 complex assessment","year":2023,"org":"CASP assessors","fam":"eval","ch":"fold","learns":"At least one successful model for 37 of 41 complex targets, where CASP14 managed 31 %","exp":"silico","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-casp15asm2023\" data-ref=\"casp15asm2023\">Ozden 2023</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"CASP16 complex assessment","year":2025,"org":"CASP assessors","fam":"eval","ch":"wet","learns":"40 oligomer targets and 13,630 models: about half are still wrong (DockQ &lt; 0.5), the gain over the baseline is not significant (p = 0.184), and on antibody and nanobody targets the baseline’s mean DockQ actually fell from 0.21 to 0.05","exp":"silico","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-casp16cplx2025\" data-ref=\"casp16cplx2025\">CASP16 complexes 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Meta-analysis of 3,766 binders","year":2025,"org":"independent meta-analysis","fam":"eval","ch":"wet","learns":"Of 3,766 published designs against 15 targets only 436 (11.6 %) actually bound; the best single predictor is AlphaFold 3’s ipSAE, yet the same metric’s precision ranges from 0.1 to 1.0 between targets, and adding more features does not generalise to unseen targets","exp":"silico","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"De novo design: state of the field","year":2026,"org":"UW IPD","fam":"eval","ch":"binder","learns":"The Baker lab’s own summary: experimentally validated binders now exist for more than 200 targets, fold design is “largely solved” and binder design “approaching being a solved problem”; what remains hard is polar targets, enzyme reactions beyond low barriers, immunogenicity and manufacturing at scale","exp":"silico","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-yang2026\" data-ref=\"yang2026\">Yang 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"A review from outside the field","year":2024,"org":"UCSF","fam":"eval","ch":"why","learns":"A more cautious review from outside the field’s centre: being able to design a structure is not the same as being able to design a function","exp":"silico","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-kortemme2024\" data-ref=\"kortemme2024\">Kortemme 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Defensive assessment of screening","year":2025,"org":"Microsoft Research, IBBIS and others","fam":"safety","ch":"safe","learns":"Run entirely in silico with nothing synthesised: assess whether screening software still recognises sequences that generative tools have rewritten, then disclose the gap to the software developers and synthesis providers first, patch it, and only then publish; the sensitive sequences were kept out of the public record","exp":"silico","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-wittmann2025\" data-ref=\"wittmann2025\">Wittmann 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Experimental assessment with stand-ins","year":2025,"org":"NIST","fam":"safety","ch":"safe","learns":"A real experiment using harmless stand-in proteins: the tools of the day could not reliably both escape recognition and keep function; the gap is mitigated, but “its true severity remains unclear”","exp":"wet","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-ikonomova2025\" data-ref=\"ikonomova2025\">Ikonomova 2025</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Responsible AI x Biodesign","year":2024,"org":"community commitment","fam":"safety","ch":"safe","learns":"A voluntary commitment by more than 170 research-group leaders, Baker among them: report concerning practices, and buy synthetic DNA only from providers that screen","exp":"claim","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-responsiblebiodesign2024\" data-ref=\"responsiblebiodesign2024\">Responsible AI x Biodesign 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"IGSC harmonised screening protocol","year":2024,"org":"International Gene Synthesis Consortium","fam":"safety","ch":"safe","learns":"Industry self-regulation: check the customer (institution and intended use) as well as the sequence, escalate flagged orders to human experts, keep records and refer cases on","exp":"claim","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-igsc2024\" data-ref=\"igsc2024\">IGSC 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"US synthesis screening framework","year":2024,"org":"US OSTP","fam":"safety","ch":"safe","learns":"Not direct regulation but funding conditions: federally funded research may order only from providers that attest to compliance, using the market to make screening the default","exp":"claim","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-ostp2024\" data-ref=\"ostp2024\">OSTP 2024</a>","url":"","urlDate":""},{"name":"Uneven global screening capacity","year":2026,"org":"RAND Europe, IBBIS","fam":"safety","ch":"safe","learns":"A peer-reviewed governance analysis: screening capacity differs sharply between countries while orders cross borders, so unilateral strictness is easy to route around","exp":"silico","weights":"","src":"<a href=\"#ref-zakaria2026\" data-ref=\"zakaria2026\">Zakaria 2026</a>","url":"","urlDate":""}],"evidence":{"wet":["Wet lab","The paper reports experimental data: expression, binding, activity or a structure"],"selfpub":["Wet lab, self-published","Wet-lab numbers exist, but only in a company’s own post or a preprint with no DOI"],"silico":["Computational only","Benchmarks and computations only, with no experimental validation"],"claim":["Statement only","A company statement with no checkable data or sequences"]},"weightKinds":{"open":["Open weights","Weights public under a permissive licence, commercial use included"],"research":["Research only","Weights public but limited to non-commercial or academic use"],"gated":["By application","Available only on application or after agreeing to terms"],"closed":["Not released","No weights; only a paper, an API or a partnership"],"seq":["Sequences only","The model is closed but the designed protein sequences were released"],"unclear":["Not stated","The public material does not say what is released or under which licence"]},"notes":{"map":"Each card is a chapter. The label gives the status of the bulk of the chapter; key points inside carry their own labels. Chapters you have opened are ticked (on this device only).","history":"Years are years of publication or official release; preprints are dated by their earliest version.","path":"To read on: <a href=\"../chem-ml/\">Chemical machine learning</a> covers Gaussian processes, Bayesian optimisation, active learning and the traps of evaluation, which is chapter 10 at length; <a href=\"../nequip/\">NequIP</a> covers equivariant neural networks, the same equivariance that protein generators need; <a href=\"../md/\">Molecular dynamics and free energy</a> covers computing binding free energies and conformational change by simulation; <a href=\"../reaction/\">Reaction kinetics and transition states</a> covers the transition-state theory and energy span of chapter 8; <a href=\"../world-models/\">World models</a> covers the more general framework of “learn a surrogate, then plan inside it”; <a href=\"../spectra/\">Spectra Hall</a> covers circular dichroism, differential scanning calorimetry and surface plasmon resonance, the measurements used to check a design.","library":"This section cites 79 papers and public documents. Everything with a DOI was checked against OpenAlex one by one (<code>node tools/pd-refs.mjs</code>); company posts and standards documents have no DOI and carry the date they were opened and read by hand."}},"refs":[{"id":"eyring1935","authors":"H. Eyring","year":1935,"title":"The Activated Complex in Chemical Reactions","journal":"The Journal of Chemical Physics","vol":"3","pages":"107–115","doi":"10.1063/1.1749604","note":"Transition-state theory and the exponential link between rate and barrier.","used":["enzyme"]},{"id":"anfinsen1973","authors":"C. B. Anfinsen","year":1973,"title":"Principles that Govern the Folding of Protein Chains","journal":"Science","vol":"181","pages":"223–230","doi":"10.1126/science.181.4096.223","note":"The origin of “sequence determines structure”: denatured ribonuclease refolds on its own.","used":["why"]},{"id":"eisenberg1984","authors":"D. Eisenberg, R. M. Weiss, T. C. Terwilliger","year":1984,"title":"The hydrophobic moment detects periodicity in protein hydrophobicity","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","vol":"81","pages":"140–144","doi":"10.1073/pnas.81.1.140","note":"The hydrophobic moment and hydrophobicity scale used by the demo of chapter 3.","used":["rosetta"]},{"id":"kauffman1989","authors":"S. A. Kauffman, E. D. Weinberger","year":1989,"title":"The NK model of rugged fitness landscapes and its application to maturation of the immune response","journal":"Journal of Theoretical Biology","vol":"141","pages":"211–245","doi":"10.1016/S0022-5193(89)80019-0","note":"The NK fitness landscape used by the demo of chapter 10.","used":["wet"]},{"id":"lau1989","authors":"K. F. Lau, K. A. Dill","year":1989,"title":"A lattice statistical mechanics model of the conformational and sequence spaces of proteins","journal":"Macromolecules","vol":"22","pages":"3986–3997","doi":"10.1021/ma00200a030","note":"The HP lattice model, used by the demo of chapter 2.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"li1996","authors":"H. Li, R. Helling, C. Tang, N. Wingreen","year":1996,"title":"Emergence of Preferred Structures in a Simple Model of Protein Folding","journal":"Science","vol":"273","pages":"666–669","doi":"10.1126/science.273.5275.666","note":"“Designability”: a few conformations are folded into by very many sequences.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"kuhlman2003","authors":"B. Kuhlman, G. Dantas, G. C. Ireton, G. Varani, B. L. Stoddard, D. Baker","year":2003,"title":"Design of a Novel Globular Protein Fold with Atomic-Level Accuracy","journal":"Science","vol":"302","pages":"1364–1368","doi":"10.1126/science.1089427","note":"Top7, the first entirely new fold designed to atomic accuracy.","used":["why","rosetta"]},{"id":"jiang2008","authors":"L. Jiang, E. A. Althoff, F. R. Clemente, et al., D. Baker","year":2008,"title":"De Novo Computational Design of Retro-Aldol Enzymes","journal":"Science","vol":"319","pages":"1387–1391","doi":"10.1126/science.1152692","note":"Retro-aldolases with accelerations up to 10⁴.","used":["rosetta"]},{"id":"rothlisberger2008","authors":"D. Röthlisberger, O. Khersonsky, A. M. Wollacott, et al., D. Baker","year":2008,"title":"Kemp elimination catalysts by computational enzyme design","journal":"Nature","vol":"453","pages":"190–195","doi":"10.1038/nature06879","note":"Eight of 59 designs had measurable activity, with accelerations up to 10⁵.","used":["rosetta"]},{"id":"romero2009","authors":"P. A. Romero, F. H. Arnold","year":2009,"title":"Exploring protein fitness landscapes by directed evolution","journal":"Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology","vol":"10","pages":"866–876","doi":"10.1038/nrm2805","note":"Why directed evolution works: fitness landscapes are locally climbable.","used":["wet"]},{"id":"marks2011","authors":"D. S. Marks, L. J. Colwell, R. Sheridan, T. A. Hopf, A. Pagnani, R. Zecchina, C. Sander","year":2011,"title":"Protein 3D Structure Computed from Evolutionary Sequence Variation","journal":"PLoS ONE","vol":"6","pages":"e28766","doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0028766","note":"Three-dimensional structure computed from the statistics of homologous sequences.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"morcos2011","authors":"F. Morcos, A. Pagnani, B. Lunt, A. Bertolino, D. S. Marks, C. Sander, R. Zecchina, M. Vendruscolo, T. Hwa, M. Weigt","year":2011,"title":"Direct-coupling analysis of residue coevolution captures native contacts across many protein families","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","vol":"108","pages":"E1293–E1301","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1111471108","note":"Direct coupling analysis: removing indirect correlations, the maths of the chapter 4 demo.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"koga2012","authors":"N. Koga, R. Tatsumi-Koga, G. Liu, R. Xiao, T. B. Acton, G. T. Montelione, D. Baker","year":2012,"title":"Principles for designing ideal protein structures","journal":"Nature","vol":"491","pages":"222–227","doi":"10.1038/nature11600","note":"Turning the rules that connect secondary structures into usable design rules.","used":["rosetta"]},{"id":"huang2016","authors":"P.-S. Huang, S. E. Boyken, D. Baker","year":2016,"title":"The coming of age of de novo protein design","journal":"Nature","vol":"537","pages":"320–327","doi":"10.1038/nature19946","note":"A survey of de novo design as it stood before deep learning.","used":["rosetta"]},{"id":"rocklin2017","authors":"G. J. Rocklin, T. M. Chidyausiku, I. Goreshnik, et al., D. Baker","year":2017,"title":"Global analysis of protein folding using massively parallel design, synthesis, and testing","journal":"Science","vol":"357","pages":"168–175","doi":"10.1126/science.aan0693","note":"Tens of thousands of designs assayed for stability at once, making design a statistical experiment.","used":["rosetta"]},{"id":"arnold2018","authors":"F. H. Arnold","year":2018,"title":"Directed Evolution: Bringing New Chemistry to Life","journal":"Angewandte Chemie International Edition","vol":"57","pages":"4143–4148","doi":"10.1002/anie.201708408","note":"Where directed evolution came from and why it works.","used":["wet"]},{"id":"yang2019","authors":"K. K. Yang, Z. Wu, F. H. Arnold","year":2019,"title":"Machine-learning-guided directed evolution for protein engineering","journal":"Nature Methods","vol":"16","pages":"687–694","doi":"10.1038/s41592-019-0496-6","note":"Machine-learning-guided directed evolution, the source of the chapter 10 demo.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"anishchenko2021","authors":"I. Anishchenko, S. J. Pellock, T. M. Chidyausiku, et al., D. Baker","year":2021,"title":"De novo protein design by deep network hallucination","journal":"Nature","vol":"600","pages":"547–552","doi":"10.1038/s41586-021-04184-w","note":"Design by “hallucination”: make the structure network satisfied with itself — the step before generative models.","used":["gen"]},{"id":"baek2021","authors":"M. Baek, F. DiMaio, I. Anishchenko, et al., D. Baker","year":2021,"title":"Accurate prediction of protein structures and interactions using a three-track neural network","journal":"Science","vol":"373","pages":"871–876","doi":"10.1126/science.abj8754","note":"RoseTTAFold, later rebuilt into the backbone of RFdiffusion.","used":["why","fold"]},{"id":"evans2021","authors":"R. Evans, M. O’Neill, A. Pritzel, et al., J. Jumper","year":2021,"title":"Protein complex prediction with AlphaFold-Multimer","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2021.10.04.463034","note":"A preprint that was never published in a journal; 70 % interface success.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"jumper2021","authors":"J. Jumper, R. Evans, A. Pritzel, et al., D. Hassabis","year":2021,"title":"Highly accurate protein structure prediction with AlphaFold","journal":"Nature","vol":"596","pages":"583–589","doi":"10.1038/s41586-021-03819-2","note":"AlphaFold2: a median backbone error of 0.96 Å at CASP14.","used":["why","fold"]},{"id":"rives2021","authors":"A. Rives, J. Meier, T. Sercu, S. Goyal, Z. Lin, J. Liu, D. Guo, M. Ott, C. L. Zitnick, J. Ma, R. Fergus","year":2021,"title":"Biological structure and function emerge from scaling unsupervised learning to 250 million protein sequences","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","vol":"118","pages":"e2016239118","doi":"10.1073/pnas.2016239118","note":"ESM-1b: structure and function emerge in a model that only ever saw sequences.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"dauparas2022","authors":"J. Dauparas, I. Anishchenko, N. Bennett, et al., D. Baker","year":2022,"title":"Robust deep learning–based protein sequence design using ProteinMPNN","journal":"Science","vol":"378","pages":"49–56","doi":"10.1126/science.add2187","note":"Inverse folding: 52.4 % recovery against Rosetta’s 32.9 %, and 200× faster.","used":["why","inverse"]},{"id":"hsu2022","authors":"C. Hsu, R. Verkuil, J. Liu, Z. Lin, B. Hie, T. Sercu, A. Lerer, A. Rives","year":2022,"title":"Learning inverse folding from millions of predicted structures","journal":"bioRxiv (ICML 2022)","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2022.04.10.487779","note":"ESM-IF1: training inverse folding on predicted structures.","used":["inverse"]},{"id":"casp15asm2023","authors":"B. Ozden, A. Kryshtafovych, E. Karaca","year":2023,"title":"The impact of AI-based modeling on the accuracy of protein assembly prediction: Insights from CASP15","journal":"Proteins","vol":"91","pages":"1636–1657","doi":"10.1002/prot.26598","note":"CASP15: at least one successful model for 37 of 41 complex targets, against 31 % at CASP14.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"cheng2023","authors":"J. Cheng, G. Novati, J. Pan, et al., P. Kohli","year":2023,"title":"Accurate proteome-wide missense variant effect prediction with AlphaMissense","journal":"Science","vol":"381","pages":"eadg7492","doi":"10.1126/science.adg7492","note":"The same class of model used to call mutations benign or pathogenic.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"gbp510","authors":"GBP510 phase 3 investigators","year":2023,"title":"Immunogenicity and safety of SARS-CoV-2 recombinant protein nanoparticle vaccine GBP510 adjuvanted with AS03: interim results of a randomised, active-controlled, observer-blinded, phase 3 trial","journal":"eClinicalMedicine","vol":"","pages":"","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498190/","note":"SKYCovione: a vaccine built on a designed icosahedral nanoparticle; interim phase 3 results in 4,036 participants with a 2.93-fold higher neutralising titre than the comparator.","used":["binder"]},{"id":"ingraham2023","authors":"J. B. Ingraham, M. Baranov, Z. Costello, et al., G. Grigoryan","year":2023,"title":"Illuminating protein space with a programmable generative model","journal":"Nature","vol":"623","pages":"1070–1078","doi":"10.1038/s41586-023-06728-8","note":"Chroma: a diffusion model that can be conditioned on symmetry, shape or text.","used":["gen"]},{"id":"lin2023","authors":"Z. Lin, H. Akin, R. Rao, et al., A. Rives","year":2023,"title":"Evolutionary-scale prediction of atomic-level protein structure with a language model","journal":"Science","vol":"379","pages":"1123–1130","doi":"10.1126/science.ade2574","note":"ESMFold: no multiple sequence alignment, just one sequence.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"nijkamp2023","authors":"E. Nijkamp, J. A. Ruffolo, E. N. Weinstein, N. Naik, A. Madani","year":2023,"title":"ProGen2: Exploring the boundaries of protein language models","journal":"Cell Systems","vol":"14","pages":"968–978","doi":"10.1016/j.cels.2023.10.002","note":"ProGen2: a protein language model that generates sequences on command.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"notin2023","authors":"P. Notin, A. W. Kollasch, D. Ritter, et al., D. S. Marks","year":2023,"title":"ProteinGym: Large-Scale Benchmarks for Protein Design and Fitness Prediction","journal":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 36","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2023.12.07.570727","note":"ProteinGym: the standard benchmark for variant-effect prediction, and the battleground of the leakage debate.","used":["plm","wet"]},{"id":"su2023","authors":"J. Su, C. Han, Y. Zhou, J. Shan, X. Zhou, F. Yuan","year":2023,"title":"SaProt: Protein Language Modeling with Structure-aware Vocabulary","journal":"bioRxiv (ICLR 2024)","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2023.10.01.560349","note":"Westlake University: fold structure into the vocabulary and train it together with sequence.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"tsuboyama2023","authors":"K. Tsuboyama, J. Dauparas, J. Chen, et al., G. J. Rocklin","year":2023,"title":"Mega-scale experimental analysis of protein folding stability in biology and design","journal":"Nature","vol":"620","pages":"434–444","doi":"10.1038/s41586-023-06328-6","note":"Folding stability measured for hundreds of thousands of sequences — a training and test set at once.","used":["rosetta"]},{"id":"watson2023","authors":"J. L. Watson, D. Juergens, N. R. Bennett, et al., D. Baker","year":2023,"title":"De novo design of protein structure and function with RFdiffusion","journal":"Nature","vol":"620","pages":"1089–1100","doi":"10.1038/s41586-023-06415-8","note":"RFdiffusion: a 19 % experimental success rate for binders and 23 of 25 motif-scaffolding problems solved.","used":["why","gen","binder"]},{"id":"yim2023","authors":"J. Yim, B. L. Trippe, V. De Bortoli, E. Mathieu, A. Doucet, R. Barzilay, T. Jaakkola","year":2023,"title":"SE(3) diffusion model with application to protein backbone generation","journal":"arXiv (ICML 2023)","vol":"","pages":"","arxiv":"2302.02277","note":"FrameDiff: diffusion of backbone frames on SE(3).","used":["gen"]},{"id":"abramson2024","authors":"J. Abramson, J. Adler, J. Dunger, et al., J. M. Jumper","year":2024,"title":"Accurate structure prediction of biomolecular interactions with AlphaFold 3","journal":"Nature","vol":"630","pages":"493–500","doi":"10.1038/s41586-024-07487-w","note":"AlphaFold 3: proteins, nucleic acids, ligands and ions in one model; weights by application only.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"boitreaud2024","authors":"Chai Discovery: J. Boitreaud, J. Dent, M. McPartlon, J. Meier, V. Reis, A. Rogozhnikov, K. Wu","year":2024,"title":"Chai-1: Decoding the molecular interactions of life","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2024.10.10.615955","note":"Chai Discovery’s structure prediction model, open for non-commercial use.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"helixfold2024","authors":"L. Liu, S. Zhang, Y. Xue, et al. (PaddleHelix Team)","year":2024,"title":"Technical Report of HelixFold3 for Biomolecular Structure Prediction","journal":"arXiv","vol":"","pages":"","arxiv":"2408.16975","note":"Baidu’s AlphaFold 3-class model; weights for non-commercial use only.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"igsc2024","authors":"International Gene Synthesis Consortium","year":2024,"title":"Harmonized Screening Protocol v3.0","journal":"genesynthesisconsortium.org","vol":"","pages":"","url":"https://genesynthesisconsortium.org/wp-content/uploads/IGSC-Harmonized-Screening-Protocol-v3.0-1.pdf","note":"The industry’s own screening protocol: check the customer as well as the sequence, and escalate flagged orders to human review.","used":["safe"]},{"id":"jiang2024","authors":"F. Jiang, M. Li, J. Dong, et al., P. Tan","year":2024,"title":"A general temperature-guided language model to design proteins of enhanced stability and activity","journal":"Science Advances","vol":"10","pages":"eadr2641","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.adr2641","note":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University and Shanghai AI Laboratory: temperature-guided enzyme redesign with wet-lab data; an erratum appeared in 2025.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"kortemme2024","authors":"T. Kortemme","year":2024,"title":"De novo protein design—From new structures to programmable functions","journal":"Cell","vol":"187","pages":"526–544","doi":"10.1016/j.cell.2023.12.028","note":"A review from outside the Baker lab, more cautious about “designing function”.","used":["why"]},{"id":"lin2024","authors":"Y. Lin, M. Lee, Z. Zhang, M. AlQuraishi","year":2024,"title":"Out of Many, One: Designing and Scaffolding Proteins at the Scale of the Structural Universe with Genie 2","journal":"arXiv","vol":"","pages":"","arxiv":"2405.15489","note":"Genie 2: an openly released backbone generator from an academic group.","used":["gen"]},{"id":"nguyen2024","authors":"E. Nguyen, M. Poli, M. G. Durrant, et al., B. L. Hie","year":2024,"title":"Sequence modeling and design from molecular to genome scale with Evo","journal":"Science","vol":"386","pages":"eado9336","doi":"10.1126/science.ado9336","note":"Evo: a genome-scale model trained on DNA.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"ostp2024","authors":"US Office of Science and Technology Policy","year":2024,"title":"Framework for Nucleic Acid Synthesis Screening","journal":"whitehouse.gov","vol":"","pages":"","url":"https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/ostp/news-updates/2024/04/29/framework-for-nucleic-acid-synthesis-screening","note":"Screening pushed through funding conditions: federally funded research may order only from providers that attest to compliance.","used":["safe"]},{"id":"responsiblebiodesign2024","authors":"Responsible AI x Biodesign signatories","year":2024,"title":"Responsible AI x Biodesign: community values, guiding principles, and commitments","journal":"responsiblebiodesign.ai","vol":"","pages":"","url":"https://responsiblebiodesign.ai/","note":"A voluntary commitment by more than 170 research-group leaders, including buying synthetic DNA only from providers that screen.","used":["safe"]},{"id":"wohlwend2024","authors":"J. Wohlwend, G. Corso, S. Passaro, et al., R. Barzilay","year":2024,"title":"Boltz-1: Democratizing Biomolecular Interaction Modeling","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2024.11.19.624167","note":"Boltz-1: an openly licensed reimplementation at AlphaFold 3 level.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"zambaldi2024","authors":"V. Zambaldi, D. La, A. E. Chu, et al., J. Wang","year":2024,"title":"De novo design of high-affinity protein binders with AlphaProteo","journal":"arXiv","vol":"","pages":"","arxiv":"2409.08022","note":"AlphaProteo: 9–88 % success on seven targets and a complete failure on TNFα; no code or weights.","used":["binder","safe"]},{"id":"bennett2025","authors":"N. R. Bennett, J. L. Watson, R. J. Ragotte, et al., D. Baker","year":2025,"title":"Atomically accurate de novo design of antibodies with RFdiffusion","journal":"Nature","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09721-5","note":"De novo antibodies: the preprint screened about 9,000 designs per target and the authors call the success rate “still quite low”.","used":["binder","wet"]},{"id":"bhatnagar2025","authors":"A. Bhatnagar, S. Jain, J. Beazer, et al., A. Madani","year":2025,"title":"Scaling Unlocks Broader Generation and Deeper Functional Understanding of Proteins","journal":"bioRxiv (NeurIPS 2025)","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2025.04.15.649055","note":"ProGen3: 46 B parameters, with 49 % of generated sequences “valid” in an expression assay.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"casp16cplx2025","authors":"J. Zhang, et al.","year":2025,"title":"Assessment of Protein Complex Predictions in CASP16: Are we making progress?","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2025.05.29.656875","note":"CASP16: about half the complex targets are still wrong, antibody–antigen worst of all.","used":["fold","wet"]},{"id":"chai2025","authors":"Chai Discovery Team: J. Boitreaud, J. Dent, D. Geisz, et al., K. Wu","year":2025,"title":"Zero-shot antibody design in a 24-well plate","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2025.07.05.663018","note":"Chai-2: a 15.5 % average antibody hit rate on 52 novel targets; weights not released.","used":["binder","safe"]},{"id":"chen2025","authors":"B. Chen, X. Cheng, P. Li, et al., L. Song","year":2025,"title":"xTrimoPGLM: unified 100-billion-parameter pretrained transformer for deciphering the language of proteins","journal":"Nature Methods","vol":"22","pages":"1028–1039","doi":"10.1038/s41592-025-02636-z","note":"BioMap and Tsinghua: a hundred-billion-parameter protein language model.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"dauparas2025","authors":"J. Dauparas, G. R. Lee, R. Pecoraro, L. An, I. Anishchenko, C. Glasscock, D. Baker","year":2025,"title":"Atomic context-conditioned protein sequence design using LigandMPNN","journal":"Nature Methods","vol":"22","pages":"717–723","doi":"10.1038/s41592-025-02626-1","note":"LigandMPNN: conditioning on the atoms of ligands, nucleic acids and metals.","used":["inverse"]},{"id":"filterbench2025","authors":"M. D. Overath, et al.","year":2025,"title":"Predicting Experimental Success in De Novo Binder Design: A Meta-Analysis of 3,766 Experimentally Characterised Binders","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2025.08.14.670059","note":"Only 11.6 % of 3,766 designs across 15 targets actually bound, and filter precision ranged from 0.1 to 1.0 between targets.","used":["why","fold","binder","wet"]},{"id":"geffner2025","authors":"T. Geffner, K. Didi, Z. Zhang, et al., K. Kreis","year":2025,"title":"Proteina: Scaling Flow-based Protein Structure Generative Models","journal":"arXiv (ICLR 2025)","vol":"","pages":"","arxiv":"2503.00710","note":"Proteina: NVIDIA’s flow-matching backbone generator.","used":["gen"]},{"id":"hayes2025","authors":"T. Hayes, R. Rao, H. Akin, et al., A. Rives","year":2025,"title":"Simulating 500 million years of evolution with a language model","journal":"Science","vol":"387","pages":"850–858","doi":"10.1126/science.ads0018","note":"ESM3 and esmGFP, which is only 58 % identical to its closest natural fluorescent protein.","used":["plm"]},{"id":"ikonomova2025","authors":"S. P. Ikonomova, B. J. Wittmann, F. Piorino, et al., G. J. Taghon","year":2025,"title":"Experimental evaluation of AI-driven protein design risks using safe biological proxies","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2025.05.15.654077","note":"NIST’s experimental assessment using harmless stand-in proteins.","used":["safe"]},{"id":"kim2025","authors":"D. Kim, S. M. Woodbury, W. Ahern, et al., D. Baker","year":2025,"title":"Computational design of metallohydrolases","journal":"Nature","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09746-w","note":"Zinc hydrolases: 5 and then 11 clearly active designs out of 96 tested per round.","used":["enzyme","wet"]},{"id":"lauko2025","authors":"A. Lauko, S. J. Pellock, K. H. Sumida, et al., D. Baker","year":2025,"title":"Computational design of serine hydrolases","journal":"Science","vol":"388","pages":"eadu2454","doi":"10.1126/science.adu2454","note":"De novo serine hydrolases, the best reaching k_cat/K_M = 2.2 × 10⁵ M⁻¹s⁻¹.","used":["enzyme"]},{"id":"openai2025retro","authors":"OpenAI and Retro Biosciences","year":2025,"title":"Accelerating life sciences research with Retro Biosciences","journal":"openai.com","vol":"","pages":"","url":"https://openai.com/index/accelerating-life-sciences-research-with-retro-biosciences/","note":"A company post with no paper behind it: GPT-4b micro redesigns SOX2 and KLF4 for a more than 50-fold increase in marker expression.","used":["why","general"]},{"id":"pacesa2025","authors":"M. Pacesa, L. Nickel, C. Schellhaas, et al., B. E. Correia","year":2025,"title":"One-shot design of functional protein binders with BindCraft","journal":"Nature","vol":"646","pages":"483–492","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09429-6","note":"BindCraft: open source, 10–100 % experimental success, and now the comparison baseline.","used":["binder"]},{"id":"passaro2025","authors":"S. Passaro, G. Corso, J. Wohlwend, et al., R. Barzilay","year":2025,"title":"Boltz-2: Towards Accurate and Efficient Binding Affinity Prediction","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2025.06.14.659707","note":"Boltz-2: MIT-licensed affinity prediction, now widely used as a filter.","used":["fold"]},{"id":"pxdesign2025","authors":"ByteDance Seed (Protenix Team)","year":2025,"title":"PXDesign: Fast, Modular, and Accurate De Novo Design of Protein Binders","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1101/2025.08.15.670450","note":"ByteDance: nanomolar hit rates of 17–82 % on six of seven targets, with TNF-α still a failure.","used":["binder"]},{"id":"ruffolo2025","authors":"J. A. Ruffolo, S. Nayfach, J. Gallagher, et al., A. Madani","year":2025,"title":"Design of highly functional genome editors by modelling CRISPR–Cas sequences","journal":"Nature","vol":"645","pages":"518–525","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09298-z","note":"OpenCRISPR-1: 403 amino acids from SpCas9, with about 95 % fewer off-target edits.","used":["general"]},{"id":"wittmann2025","authors":"B. J. Wittmann, T. Alexanian, C. M. Bartling, et al., E. Horvitz","year":2025,"title":"Strengthening nucleic acid biosecurity screening against generative protein design tools","journal":"Science","vol":"390","pages":"82–87","doi":"10.1126/science.adu8578","note":"A defensive assessment of synthesis-screening software, patched before publication.","used":["safe"]},{"id":"ahern2026","authors":"W. Ahern, J. Yim, D. Tischer, et al., D. Baker","year":2026,"title":"Atom-level enzyme active site scaffolding using RFdiffusion2","journal":"Nature Methods","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1038/s41592-025-02975-x","note":"RFdiffusion2: active sites specified atom by atom, 41 of 41 on the benchmark against RFdiffusion’s 16.","used":["gen","enzyme"]},{"id":"brixi2026","authors":"G. Brixi, M. G. Durrant, J. Ku, et al., B. L. Hie","year":2026,"title":"Genome modelling and design across all domains of life with Evo 2","journal":"Nature","vol":"652","pages":"1349–1361","doi":"10.1038/s41586-026-10176-5","note":"Evo 2: nine trillion bases and a million-base context, with weights and data released.","used":["plm","general"]},{"id":"candido2026","authors":"S. Candido, T. Hayes, A. Derry, et al., A. Rives","year":2026,"title":"Language Modeling Materializes a World Model of Protein Biology","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.64898/2026.06.03.729735","note":"ESMC and ESMFold2: a 50 % antibody–antigen DockQ pass rate from sequence alone, and wet-lab minibinder hit rates of 36–88 %.","used":["plm","binder"]},{"id":"chen2026","authors":"A. Chen, K. Wu, H. Choi, et al., D. Baker","year":2026,"title":"Computational design of metalloproteases","journal":"Nature","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1038/s41586-026-11167-2","note":"Metalloproteases: 36 % of 135 designs cut the peptide at the intended bond.","used":["enzyme"]},{"id":"dai2026","authors":"F. Dai, S. You, Y. Zhu, et al., F. Yuan","year":2026,"title":"Programmable design of functional proteins from natural language","journal":"Nature","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.1038/s41586-026-11205-z","note":"Pinal (Westlake University): order a protein in plain language; 1 of 29 for GFP, 1 of 94 for a PETase, 5 of 5 for an alcohol dehydrogenase.","used":["gen","general"]},{"id":"didi2026","authors":"K. Didi, Z. Zhang, G. Zhou, et al., K. Kreis","year":2026,"title":"Scaling Atomistic Protein Binder Design with Generative Pretraining and Test-Time Compute","journal":"arXiv (ICLR 2026)","vol":"","pages":"","arxiv":"2603.27950","note":"NVIDIA: atomistic binder design with generative pretraining and test-time compute.","used":["binder"]},{"id":"gpt6preprint","authors":"OpenAI","year":2026,"title":"GPT-6 as a Universal Foundation Model for Protein Biology: Beyond Specialized Models","journal":"cdn.openai.com (self-hosted preprint)","vol":"","pages":"","url":"https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/gpt-6-a-universal-foundation-model-for-protein-biology.pdf","note":"A self-hosted PDF with no DOI, no preprint-server identifier and no named authors; the numbers in this section are taken from the company’s own page.","used":["general"]},{"id":"kong2026","authors":"X. Kong, J. Chen, Z. Zhang, et al., J. Ma","year":2026,"title":"Programming Biomolecular Interactions with All-Atom Generative Model","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.64898/2026.03.12.711044","note":"Tsinghua, ByteDance and others: one all-atom model for peptides, nanobodies and small molecules, with wet-lab data on KRAS G12D and PCSK9.","used":["gen","binder"]},{"id":"li2026","authors":"Y. Li, Y. Jing, Y. Su, W. Ren, J. Zhang, T. Liu","year":2026,"title":"De novo protein design in an expanded chemical space","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.64898/2026.10.08.757794","note":"Peking University: design around non-canonical amino acids and post-translational modifications, with per-campaign counts reported.","used":["gen","binder"]},{"id":"openai2026gpt6","authors":"OpenAI","year":2026,"title":"GPT-6 models demonstrate intrinsic protein-design capabilities","journal":"openai.com","vol":"","pages":"","url":"https://openai.com/index/gpt-6-models-demonstrate-intrinsic-protein-design-capabilities/","note":"The company post of 24 November 2026: 35 of 322 minibinders (10.9 %) bound; it links a preprint with no DOI.","used":["why","fold","inverse","binder","general"]},{"id":"wu2026","authors":"Z. Wu, J. Abramson, T. Frerix, et al., J. Wang","year":2026,"title":"Designing enzymes for new-to-nature chemistry and non-natural substrates with AlphaProtein Novo","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.64898/2026.10.01.756017","note":"DeepMind with the Arnold lab: Kemp eliminase hit rates from 3 % to 34 %, esterases from 0 % to 44 %.","used":["enzyme"]},{"id":"yang2026","authors":"W. Yang, S. Wang, G. R. Lee, et al., D. Baker","year":2026,"title":"The past, present and future of de novo protein design","journal":"Nature","vol":"652","pages":"1139–1152","doi":"10.1038/s41586-026-10328-7","note":"The Baker lab’s own summary: binder design is “approaching being a solved problem”, while polar targets and enzyme activity remain hard.","used":["why","binder"]},{"id":"zakaria2026","authors":"S. Zakaria, S. Peresson, C. M. Sharkey, K. Engel","year":2026,"title":"Compounding asymmetries in nucleic acid synthesis screening: policy pathways for strengthening global biosecurity governance","journal":"Frontiers in Bioengineering and Biotechnology","vol":"14","pages":"1819575","doi":"10.3389/fbioe.2026.1819575","note":"The uneven distribution of screening capacity between countries, and the policy paths open.","used":["safe"]},{"id":"zhang2026","authors":"Y. Zhang, C. Gong, J. Sun, et al., W. Xiao","year":2026,"title":"Protenix-v2: Broadening the Reach of Structure Prediction and Biomolecular Design","journal":"bioRxiv","vol":"","pages":"","doi":"10.64898/2026.04.10.717613","note":"ByteDance: both code and weights under Apache-2.0, the most permissive AlphaFold 3-class release from a Chinese group.","used":["fold"]}],"chapters":{"why":{"id":"why","body":[{"p":"A chain of 100 amino acids has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>130</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> possible sequences. There are about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>80</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> atoms in the observable universe, so that number is not “large” but physically meaningless. The first thing any design method has to answer is how to find a usable sequence inside a space that size."},{"h":"The licence Anfinsen gave us"},{"p":"In 1973 Anfinsen summarised a series of denaturation–renaturation experiments on ribonuclease: under physiological conditions the native conformation of a chain is determined uniquely by its amino-acid sequence, as the state of lowest free energy <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-anfinsen1973\" data-ref=\"anfinsen1973\">Anfinsen 1973</a>]</span>. That sentence is the licence for all of protein design — if structure is a function of sequence, then specifying a structure is in principle the same as writing the right sequence."},{"p":"But “in principle” hides two problems. First, the function has no closed form: you either run an experiment or solve a very hard optimisation. Second, it is not invertible: many sequences fold into almost the same structure, so the inverse problem has infinitely many solutions, and most of them fail on something else — expression, solubility, aggregation, immunogenicity. Design is not finding a unique inverse but picking, out of an enormous solution set, one that passes on every count."},{"h":"Two quite different problems"},{"table":{"head":["","Structure prediction","Design"],"rows":[["Given","A sequence","A structure, a function, or a sentence"],["Wanted","Three-dimensional coordinates","One sequence, or a batch"],["Unique answer?","Essentially yes, apart from flexibility and polymorphism","No: the solution set is enormous"],["How do you know it worked?","Compare with a crystal or cryo-EM structure","Only by experiment: does it express, fold, bind, work?"],["Cost of being wrong","One wrong model","A batch of wasted experiments, tens of thousands of dollars per target"]],"cap":"The two are often conflated. Prediction can check itself on a computer; design cannot — a distinction every later chapter returns to."}},{"h":"Three generations of answers"},{"steps":["<b>Physics-based scoring plus search</b> (1990s–2010s). Write an energy function and run Monte Carlo over sequence and side-chain conformations. Top7 in 2003 is this generation’s landmark: a fold with no natural counterpart, 1.17 Å from the design in the crystal <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kuhlman2003\" data-ref=\"kuhlman2003\">Kuhlman 2003</a>]</span>. The price is that errors in the energy function, a few kcal/mol, turn straight into design failures (<a href=\"#c-rosetta\" data-open=\"rosetta\">03 · Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved</a>).","<b>Deep learning takes over the judging and the writing</b> (2021–). AlphaFold2 and RoseTTAFold made structure prediction good enough to referee <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jumper2021\" data-ref=\"jumper2021\">Jumper 2021</a>; <a href=\"#ref-baek2021\" data-ref=\"baek2021\">Baek 2021</a>]</span>; ProteinMPNN writes sequences for a backbone better and faster than Rosetta <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a>]</span>; diffusion models such as RFdiffusion generate backbones directly <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a>]</span>. The pipeline of this generation is generate, write sequences, score with a predictor, send a few dozen to the bench.","<b>General-purpose models push in</b> (2025–). In 2025 OpenAI redesigned two reprogramming factors with a small model specialised for protein engineering — a company post, with no paper <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2025retro\" data-ref=\"openai2025retro\">OpenAI 2025 (company post)</a>]</span>; in November 2026 it reported that GPT-6-class models design binders without a specialised design model <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>]</span>. The boundaries of this generation are not drawn yet; <a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a> reads its numbers and its own stated limits."]},{"note":"<b>This section contains no protocols.</b> Everything here is published or publicly announced results and the concepts behind them. It does not deal with toxins, pathogens or any harmful protein, and it gives no laboratory procedures. The last chapter describes how screening and governance work (<a href=\"#c-safe\" data-open=\"safe\">11 · Biosecurity: keeping screening in step with the models</a>).","kind":"warn"},{"h":"First, feel how big the space is"},{"p":"The demo below puts two numbers side by side: the whole sequence space <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, and how many sequences lie inside a ball of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math> mutations from a known sequence. The ball is the range in which directed evolution and most real protein engineering actually operate."},{"demo":"seqspace"},{"p":"Both numbers are exact — the binomials are computed in integer arithmetic — so one conclusion can be read off directly: the part you can make experimentally is always a vanishing corner of the space, and the entire value of a method lies in moving that corner to the right place."},{"h":"Who is doing this"},{"p":"Published results come from roughly four kinds of institution: academic laboratories (the Baker lab at the University of Washington is the densest single source, and most of <a href=\"#c-gen\" data-open=\"gen\">06 · Generating backbones: how a diffusion model grows a protein</a> and <a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a> comes from there), large AI laboratories (Google DeepMind, Meta / EvolutionaryScale, OpenAI, NVIDIA, ByteDance, Baidu), companies built specifically around proteins (Profluent, Generate Biomedicines, Chai Discovery, the BindCraft group out of EPFL), and several Chinese universities and companies (Westlake, Tsinghua, Peking University, SJTU, BioMap). The results table on the front page lists them by category with a level of evidence on every row."},{"p":"One caution: the narrative of this field is written mostly by the people producing the results. The Baker lab’s 2026 review calls fold design “largely solved” and binder design “approaching being a solved problem” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yang2026\" data-ref=\"yang2026\">Yang 2026</a>]</span>, while a review from outside the field’s centre is far more careful about designing function <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kortemme2024\" data-ref=\"kortemme2024\">Kortemme 2024</a>]</span>, and an independent meta-analysis found that only 11.6 % of 3,766 published binder designs actually bound <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a>]</span>. Both can be true at once: the list of things that can be done has grown, and the average success rate is still low."}],"points":[{"h":"Design is an inverse problem, and its solution is not unique","p":"Many sequences fold into the same structure, so design is picking from a solution set rather than inverting a function. This is also why “sequence recovery” is a misleading metric (<a href=\"#c-inverse\" data-open=\"inverse\">05 · Inverse folding: writing a sequence for a backbone</a>).","st":"proved"},{"h":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is not “large”, it is physically meaningless","p":"100 residues already give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>130</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> sequences. Every method is at bottom a way of restricting the search to a far smaller subset: a mutational ball, a structural condition, or a learned distribution.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Prediction can check itself; design cannot","p":"A prediction can be compared with an experimental structure; a design can only be tested at the bench. “Better on a benchmark” and “a higher wet-lab success rate” are therefore different claims, quantified in <a href=\"#c-wet\" data-open=\"wet\">10 · How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars</a>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Anfinsen’s conclusion has limits","p":"Sequence determines structure under conditions: physiological buffer, no chaperone, thermodynamic control. Membrane proteins, intrinsically disordered regions, chaperone-dependent folding and amyloid aggregation all sit outside those conditions.","st":"approx"},{"h":"The field’s narrative and the independent statistics disagree","p":"“Approaching solved” and “11.6 % actually bound” are both 2026 statements. Any success rate has to be read together with the target, the number of designs and the filtering used.","st":"disputed"},{"h":"Why a chemist should care","p":"The whole pattern — generate candidates, filter most of them with a cheap model, validate a few experimentally, feed the result back — is the same loop as computation-driven discovery of materials and catalysts. The bridge table on the front page lines them up row by row.","st":"history"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.27</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>130</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The size of sequence space. For comparison, all proteins in all life on Earth number something like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>12</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>9</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The number of sequences within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math> mutations of a known one: each changed position has 19 alternatives. This is what the demo computes."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></munder><mtext>  </mtext><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></munder><mtext>  </mtext><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>c</mi></munder><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The correct objective of design: not minimising the energy of the target structure (positive design) but maximising its probability among all conformations. The competing conformations in the denominator have to be pushed up, which is what “negative design” means."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>fold</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>folded</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>unfolded</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>most natural proteins:</mtext><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>∼</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn><mtext> kcal/mol</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The scale of folding free energy; most natural proteins sit between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol. A hydrogen bond is worth 1–2 kcal/mol, so a whole chain’s stability is a few hydrogen bonds’ worth, and a 2 kcal/mol error in a scoring function can flip the conclusion."}],"example":{"q":"For a 100-residue protein, how many sequences lie within at most 5 substitutions? What fraction of the whole sequence space is that?","steps":["Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>100</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>9</mn><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> term by term: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives 1, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>19</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1900</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4950</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>361</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>786</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>950</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>161</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>700</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>6859</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.109</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>921</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>225</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>130</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>321</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5.110</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>287</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>520</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>476</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>099</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.864</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>14</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. The last term is 99.7 % of the total — the usual shape of a combinatorial explosion.","Summing: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.869</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>14</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> sequences, against <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.268</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>130</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> for the whole space — a ratio of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>116</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","Compare with experimental capacity: the largest display libraries screen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> sequences at once, so even this “only five substitutions” ball cannot be exhausted."],"a":"About <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.87</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>14</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> sequences, a fraction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>116</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> of the space. The conclusion cuts both ways: local search cannot exhaust even a tiny neighbourhood, so a model is needed to choose; and at the same time there are already tens of trillions of candidates within five steps of the natural sequence, so “the model suggests more than we can test” was never the bottleneck — choosing well is."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-anfinsen1973\" data-ref=\"anfinsen1973\">Anfinsen 1973</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kuhlman2003\" data-ref=\"kuhlman2003\">Kuhlman 2003</a> · <a href=\"#ref-baek2021\" data-ref=\"baek2021\">Baek 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-jumper2021\" data-ref=\"jumper2021\">Jumper 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kortemme2024\" data-ref=\"kortemme2024\">Kortemme 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-openai2025retro\" data-ref=\"openai2025retro\">OpenAI 2025 (company post)</a> · <a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a> · <a href=\"#ref-yang2026\" data-ref=\"yang2026\">Yang 2026</a>"},"fold":{"id":"fold","body":[{"p":"Levinthal did the arithmetic in 1969: with three conformations per residue, 100 residues give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>47</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> states, and sampling one per picosecond would take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>28</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> years. Real proteins fold in milliseconds to seconds. So folding is not an enumeration but a slide down a funnel, with energy and conformational entropy falling together and no need to search the whole space."},{"h":"The smallest model: an HP lattice"},{"p":"In 1989 Lau and Dill compressed amino acids into two kinds, hydrophobic (H) and polar (P), put the chain on a two-dimensional square lattice, and counted energy as the number of H–H pairs that are lattice neighbours but not chain neighbours <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lau1989\" data-ref=\"lau1989\">Lau &amp; Dill 1989</a>]</span>. The model is simple enough that for short chains <b>every</b> conformation and <b>every</b> sequence can be enumerated, which answers a question no real system can: how many sequences actually have a unique ground state?"},{"demo":"hp"},{"p":"The demo enumerates every self-avoiding walk up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></semantics></math> there are 15,037 inequivalent conformations, matching OEIS A001411) and then folds all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math> sequences. Two conclusions are worth keeping:"},{"list":["<b>Folding is easy, design is hard.</b> Finding the ground state of a given sequence is one search; but a sequence written at random most likely has no unique ground state (only a few per cent do at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></semantics></math>), so design has to work backwards and select sequences.","<b>Designability is wildly uneven.</b> A few conformations are each folded into by dozens of sequences, while most conformations have none at all <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-li1996\" data-ref=\"li1996\">Li 1996</a>]</span>. The small number of natural folds — on the order of a thousand topologies in CATH — is plausibly this effect writ large."]},{"h":"What happened in 2021"},{"p":"At CASP14, AlphaFold2 brought the median backbone error of single-chain prediction to 0.96 Å against 2.8 Å for the runner-up <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jumper2021\" data-ref=\"jumper2021\">Jumper 2021</a>]</span>; RoseTTAFold reached a comparable level at the same time with a three-track network, and was open source <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-baek2021\" data-ref=\"baek2021\">Baek 2021</a>]</span>. For design the significance is not that structures are finally known, but that there is now <b>a cheap referee</b>: generate a sequence, predict its structure, and see whether it comes back as the shape you wanted. This self-consistency check became the sieve in every later pipeline (<a href=\"#c-inverse\" data-open=\"inverse\">05 · Inverse folding: writing a sequence for a backbone</a>, <a href=\"#c-gen\" data-open=\"gen\">06 · Generating backbones: how a diffusion model grows a protein</a>)."},{"p":"Complexes are much harder than single chains. AlphaFold-Multimer reported a 70 % heteromeric interface success rate on 4,446 complexes at DockQ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo></mrow></semantics></math> 0.23, a low bar <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-evans2021\" data-ref=\"evans2021\">Evans 2021</a>]</span>; the CASP15 assessment found at least one successful model for 37 of 41 complex targets, where CASP14 managed 31 % <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-casp15asm2023\" data-ref=\"casp15asm2023\">Ozden 2023</a>]</span>. By 2024 AlphaFold 3 put proteins, nucleic acids, ligands and ions into one diffusion model <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-abramson2024\" data-ref=\"abramson2024\">Abramson 2024</a>]</span>; its code opened at the end of that year but its weights require an application and are non-commercial, which made open reimplementations a necessity: Boltz-1 and the affinity-predicting Boltz-2 from MIT <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wohlwend2024\" data-ref=\"wohlwend2024\">Wohlwend 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-passaro2025\" data-ref=\"passaro2025\">Passaro 2025</a>]</span>, Chai-1 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-boitreaud2024\" data-ref=\"boitreaud2024\">Chai-1 2024</a>]</span>, Baidu’s HelixFold3 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-helixfold2024\" data-ref=\"helixfold2024\">HelixFold3 2024</a>]</span>, and ByteDance’s fully Apache-2.0 Protenix-v2 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zhang2026\" data-ref=\"zhang2026\">Protenix-v2 2026</a>]</span>."},{"note":"These reimplementations are not academic courtesy but the infrastructure of design. OpenAI’s own post of November 2026 states that candidates generated by GPT-6 were filtered with Boltz-2, OpenDDE and ESMFold2 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>]</span> — so even in a pipeline where a general model does the designing, the referee is still a specialised model.","kind":"info"},{"h":"Three blind spots of the referee"},{"steps":["<b>It does not judge manufacturability.</b> A predictor will happily return a beautiful structure for a sequence that will aggregate into inclusion bodies in <i>E. coli</i>. Expression and solubility still have no reliable predictor.","<b>Its confidence at interfaces is poorly calibrated.</b> The same scoring metric can have a precision anywhere from 0.1 to 1.0 depending on the target <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a>]</span>, so one threshold does not transfer between targets.","<b>It returns one conformation, not an ensemble.</b> Flexibility, disorder, allostery and conformational change on binding are all outside its output; those need molecular dynamics or enhanced sampling (<a href=\"../md/\">Molecular dynamics and free energy</a>)."]},{"p":"One sentence for this chapter: prediction solved looking, design needs choosing. The next six chapters are all about choosing."}],"points":[{"h":"Folding is a funnel, not a search","p":"Levinthal’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>28</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> years rules out enumeration; it is the overall tilt of the energy surface towards the native state — the funnel — that makes millisecond folding possible. It is also why design has to push the competing conformations up at the same time.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Uneven designability can be proved in a toy model","p":"Once every sequence and conformation on an HP lattice is enumerated, conformations folded into by many sequences and conformations folded into by none appear together <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-li1996\" data-ref=\"li1996\">Li 1996</a>]</span>. That is an exactly computable fact, not a metaphor.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The value of prediction is as a referee","p":"AlphaFold2’s 0.96 Å turned “generate, predict, compare” into a cheap sieve, and only then did the whole design pipeline become possible <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jumper2021\" data-ref=\"jumper2021\">Jumper 2021</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Complexes are still the weak spot","p":"“Success” at DockQ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo></mrow></semantics></math> 0.23 only means part of the interface is right; in the CASP16 oligomer assessment about half of the predictions are still wrong (<a href=\"#c-wet\" data-open=\"wet\">10 · How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-casp16cplx2025\" data-ref=\"casp16cplx2025\">CASP16 complexes 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"Open reimplementations are design infrastructure","p":"With AlphaFold 3’s weights closed, Boltz, Chai-1, HelixFold3 and Protenix took that place, and nearly every published design pipeline now uses one of them as its filter.","st":"history"},{"h":"A predictor will not tell you whether it can be made","p":"Expression, solubility, aggregation and immunogenicity are not in its output, and they are among the main reasons designs actually fail.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>search</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> ps</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Levinthal’s estimate. The exact base of the exponent hardly matters; the order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>47</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is robust."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mi>δ</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The HP lattice energy: count only H–H pairs that are lattice neighbours and not chain neighbours, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. This is the expression the demo uses."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><munder><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></munder><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mtext> , unique</mtext><mo fence=\"true\">}</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The designability of a conformation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: how many sequences have it as their unique ground state. A few conformations have large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; most have zero <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-li1996\" data-ref=\"li1996\">Li 1996</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>scRMSD</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mrow><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> predict</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>design</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo><mover><mo><mo>&lt;</mo></mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">?</mo></mover></mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>A</mtext><mo>˚</mo></mover></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The self-consistency check: predict the designed sequence again and see whether it returns to the target structure. This has been the standard sieve in every pipeline since 2021, but it is only a necessary condition."}],"example":{"q":"Under Levinthal’s assumptions, how long would a 100-residue chain take to try every conformation? And if each residue had only two conformations?","steps":["Three per residue: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>5.15</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>47</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","At 1 ps per conformation: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.15</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>47</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mtext>s</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>5.15</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>35</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>.","A year is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.156</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> s, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>28</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> years, against <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> years for the age of the universe.","With two per residue: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>100</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.27</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>30</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> years — still longer than the age of the universe."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>28</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> years, and even with two conformations per residue, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> years. Dropping the base from 3 to 2 saves only 17 orders of magnitude while real folding takes milliseconds, which shows the problem was never the speed of trying but that the energy surface itself points the way. Design has to <b>build such a surface</b>, which is harder than prediction, because the surface is a function of the sequence and the sequence is what we are looking for."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-lau1989\" data-ref=\"lau1989\">Lau &amp; Dill 1989</a> · <a href=\"#ref-li1996\" data-ref=\"li1996\">Li 1996</a> · <a href=\"#ref-baek2021\" data-ref=\"baek2021\">Baek 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-evans2021\" data-ref=\"evans2021\">Evans 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-jumper2021\" data-ref=\"jumper2021\">Jumper 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-casp15asm2023\" data-ref=\"casp15asm2023\">Ozden 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-abramson2024\" data-ref=\"abramson2024\">Abramson 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-boitreaud2024\" data-ref=\"boitreaud2024\">Chai-1 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-helixfold2024\" data-ref=\"helixfold2024\">HelixFold3 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wohlwend2024\" data-ref=\"wohlwend2024\">Wohlwend 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-casp16cplx2025\" data-ref=\"casp16cplx2025\">CASP16 complexes 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-passaro2025\" data-ref=\"passaro2025\">Passaro 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zhang2026\" data-ref=\"zhang2026\">Protenix-v2 2026</a>"},"rosetta":{"id":"rosetta","body":[{"p":"Before deep learning, design worked like this: fix a target backbone (or build one from rules), then run Monte Carlo over which amino acid sits at each position and how its side chain is posed, scoring with an energy function made of a few dozen terms. The terms are familiar chemistry: Lennard-Jones repulsion and attraction, directional hydrogen bonds, an implicit solvation term, backbone and side-chain conformational preferences from statistical rotamer libraries, and electrostatics."},{"h":"Top7: a fold nature never made"},{"p":"In 2003 Kuhlman and colleagues designed a 93-residue α/β protein whose topology had no counterpart in the structural databases. Its crystal structure sits 1.17 Å from the design model, and its circular-dichroism spectrum at 98 °C is almost identical to the one at 25 °C — it does not unfold near boiling <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kuhlman2003\" data-ref=\"kuhlman2003\">Kuhlman 2003</a>]</span>. That single result established two things: the physics of sequence and structure can be used to <b>build</b>, and what is built can be extremely stable."},{"p":"The next decade was the decade of rules. In 2012 Koga and colleagues turned “which loop lengths and chiralities join two secondary-structure elements into a given unit” into a rule set, so one could draw a blueprint first — which helices, which strands, how long the loops — and let the program fill in the sequence <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-koga2012\" data-ref=\"koga2012\">Koga 2012</a>]</span>. By the time the field was reviewed in 2016, the designable repertoire already included ideal folds, helical bundles, small binders and self-assembling symmetric cages <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huang2016\" data-ref=\"huang2016\">Huang 2016</a>]</span>."},{"h":"One rule explains half of these designs"},{"p":"An α helix turns about 100° per residue, so seven residues make nearly two full turns (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>72</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>102.</mn><msup><mn>9</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>). Put the hydrophobic residues at the a and d positions of the heptad abcdefg and they all land on one face of the helix; glue several such faces together and you have a bundle. Eisenberg quantified this in 1984 as the hydrophobic moment <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eisenberg1984\" data-ref=\"eisenberg1984\">Eisenberg 1984</a>]</span>, and the demo below plots it."},{"demo":"bundle"},{"p":"Sweeping the degrees per residue shows it: the heptad’s hydrophobic moment peaks near 102.8° (the 28-residue sequence in the demo gives 102.8° with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.565</mn></mrow></semantics></math>), while an alternating hydrophobic–polar sequence peaks at 180°, which is a β strand. The same Fourier-style peak analysis is used in <a href=\"../chemistry/physical/\">physical chemistry</a> to judge the amphiphilicity of surfactants and is the selection rule of diffraction in <a href=\"../optics/\">optics</a> — one piece of mathematics."},{"h":"Structure could be designed; activity could not"},{"p":"In 2008, papers in <i>Nature</i> and <i>Science</i> designed Kemp eliminases and retro-aldolases. The result cut both ways: for the Kemp eliminase, 59 designs on 17 scaffolds gave 8 with measurable activity and accelerations up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> over the uncatalysed reaction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rothlisberger2008\" data-ref=\"rothlisberger2008\">Röthlisberger 2008</a>]</span>; the retro-aldolases hit with all four catalytic motifs, reached <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, and gave crystal structures matching the designs in two scaffolds <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jiang2008\" data-ref=\"jiang2008\">Jiang 2008</a>]</span>. Against natural enzymes those numbers are still four to six orders of magnitude short. More telling: the Kemp eliminase was later improved 200-fold by directed evolution — the design provided a starting point and the rest of the gain came from experiment."},{"p":"Why could structure be designed and activity not? Structure only requires the energy surface to have a deep enough minimum, whereas catalysis requires the <b>transition state</b> to be stabilised more than the substrate, with a margin of 1–2 kcal/mol (<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a> writes the arithmetic out). On a 2 kcal/mol scale the scoring functions are simply not reliable."},{"h":"In 2017 design became a statistical experiment"},{"p":"Rocklin and colleagues measured the stability of tens of thousands of designs at once <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rocklin2017\" data-ref=\"rocklin2017\">Rocklin 2017</a>]</span>. That changed the nature of the field: before it, a paper reported a handful of designs and success was hard to argue about; after it, one could plot predicted score against measured stability, so the error of a scoring function was quantified for the first time, and could be improved with data. The later dataset of folding free energies for hundreds of thousands of sequences <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tsuboyama2023\" data-ref=\"tsuboyama2023\">Tsuboyama 2023</a>]</span> continues the same path and is now the training set for stability predictors."},{"h":"An inherited flaw: too stable"},{"p":"That Top7 survives boiling sounds like a virtue, but it is also a symptom: the objective was maximal stability, so designed proteins are generally far more stable than their natural counterparts, often with melting temperatures above 90 °C. Natural proteins are not like that — they need flexibility (for allostery and catalysis) and degradability (to be regulated). The demo below converts extra stability into melting temperature."},{"demo":"stab"},{"note":"There is a general lesson in the demo: the conversion from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, so “only 1 kcal/mol more” is already 3.5 K. Note also the diminishing-returns slider — real stabilising mutations always add up to less than their sum (epistasis), one of the most reliable rules in protein engineering, and it returns in the directed-evolution demo of <a href=\"#c-wet\" data-open=\"wet\">10 · How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars</a>.","kind":"info"},{"p":"Deep learning did not discard this generation; it replaced two components in its workflow: the scoring (now a predictor’s self-consistency check, <a href=\"#c-fold\" data-open=\"fold\">02 · Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design</a>) and the sequence writing (now an inverse-folding network, <a href=\"#c-inverse\" data-open=\"inverse\">05 · Inverse folding: writing a sequence for a backbone</a>). The shape of the pipeline — fix a structure, fill in a sequence, filter, go to the bench — is still the one set in the Rosetta era."}],"points":[{"h":"Top7 proved a new fold can be built","p":"93 residues, a topology absent from nature, 1.17 Å from the design in the crystal, and stable in boiling water <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kuhlman2003\" data-ref=\"kuhlman2003\">Kuhlman 2003</a>]</span>. This is the field’s founding fact.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Blueprint design turned geometry into rules","p":"Loop length and chirality decide which units can be joined <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-koga2012\" data-ref=\"koga2012\">Koga 2012</a>]</span>, so the topology can be drawn before the sequence is filled in. Generative models are learning the same rules, only without anyone writing them down.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The heptad repeat is an exact periodicity result","p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>72</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>102.</mn><msup><mn>9</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> matches the per-residue rotation of an α helix, so hydrophobic residues at a and d share a face. The demo locates the peak exactly.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Activity is a different barrier, and it is a question of energy scale","p":"The 2008 designed enzymes were four to six orders of magnitude slower than natural ones <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rothlisberger2008\" data-ref=\"rothlisberger2008\">Röthlisberger 2008</a>; <a href=\"#ref-jiang2008\" data-ref=\"jiang2008\">Jiang 2008</a>]</span>, because catalysis demands 1–2 kcal/mol accuracy and the scoring functions do not provide it.","st":"tested"},{"h":"High-throughput measurement made design an improvable engineering discipline","p":"Once tens of thousands of designs could be measured at once, the error of a scoring function became measurable and correctable with data <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rocklin2017\" data-ref=\"rocklin2017\">Rocklin 2017</a>; <a href=\"#ref-tsuboyama2023\" data-ref=\"tsuboyama2023\">Tsuboyama 2023</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"“Too stable” is a side effect of the objective","p":"Design selects the most stable sequence while natural proteins compromise between stability and function. There is still no method for designing a protein that is deliberately only just stable enough.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>rep</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>atr</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>sol</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>elec</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>hb</mtext></msub><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>rot</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>bb</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The skeleton of a Rosetta-style energy: pairwise terms plus one-body terms, each with a weight fitted to known structures. It is therefore not a first-principles energy but a trained scoring function."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mfrac><mrow><mn>72</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>102.8</mn><msup><mn>6</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Eisenberg’s hydrophobic moment <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eisenberg1984\" data-ref=\"eisenberg1984\">Eisenberg 1984</a>]</span>: sum the hydrophobicities as vectors pointing at their azimuths on the helix. The demo sweeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>unfold</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Gibbs–Helmholtz for two-state folding; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> comes from water reorganising around an exposed hydrophobic core, and it is the term that makes the curve unfold at both high and low temperature (cold denaturation)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>small </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The small-perturbation conversion: at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>330</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol, 1 kcal/mol buys 3.67 K (exact value 3.56 K)."}],"example":{"q":"A small domain with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>330</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K (57 °C) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol takes four stabilising mutations worth 0.8 kcal/mol each. Where does the melting temperature end up — and what if each one only delivers 80 % of the previous?","steps":["Ideal additivity: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3.2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol, and the approximation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>330</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>90</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>11.7</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K.","With the discount <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>0.8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.</mn><msup><mn>8</mn><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.64</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.512</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2.36</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol, which is 74 % of the ideal.","Solving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>unfold</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/(mol·K) gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>338.1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8.11</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K; the approximation gives 8.66 K, an overestimate of 7 %.","In degrees: 57 °C becomes 65 °C, at an average of 3.44 K per kcal/mol."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>65</mn></mrow></semantics></math> °C (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8.1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K), not the 69 °C of ideal additivity. Two magnitudes to remember: <b>1 kcal/mol buys about 3.5 K of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math></b>, so the “5–10 kcal/mol more stable than natural” that designs routinely achieve is 20–35 K, which is where those 90 °C-plus melting temperatures come from; and <b>epistasis discounts the stacking</b>, so “find ten mutations worth 1 kcal/mol each and gain 10 kcal/mol” never holds."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-eisenberg1984\" data-ref=\"eisenberg1984\">Eisenberg 1984</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kuhlman2003\" data-ref=\"kuhlman2003\">Kuhlman 2003</a> · <a href=\"#ref-jiang2008\" data-ref=\"jiang2008\">Jiang 2008</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rothlisberger2008\" data-ref=\"rothlisberger2008\">Röthlisberger 2008</a> · <a href=\"#ref-koga2012\" data-ref=\"koga2012\">Koga 2012</a> · <a href=\"#ref-huang2016\" data-ref=\"huang2016\">Huang 2016</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rocklin2017\" data-ref=\"rocklin2017\">Rocklin 2017</a> · <a href=\"#ref-tsuboyama2023\" data-ref=\"tsuboyama2023\">Tsuboyama 2023</a>"},"plm":{"id":"plm","body":[{"p":"Evolution left us a free dataset: thousands of sequences in one protein family, with essentially the same structure. If positions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> touch in space, then when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> grows, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> has to shrink or it will not fit — and the trace left by such compensating mutations is coevolution. People tried to read contact maps off alignments in the 1990s and it never worked well, until two papers in 2011 explained why: correlation is transitive."},{"h":"Why you have to invert"},{"p":"If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></semantics></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> correlate too, even half a protein apart. Picking out the direct couplings has a mathematically clean answer: the true couplings are the nonzero entries of the <b>precision matrix</b> (the inverse covariance), while transitive correlation lives only in the covariance. Invert the observed covariance and the indirect correlations vanish by themselves. That is direct coupling analysis, DCA <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marks2011\" data-ref=\"marks2011\">Marks 2011</a>; <a href=\"#ref-morcos2011\" data-ref=\"morcos2011\">Morcos 2011</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"msa"},{"p":"The demo uses a Gaussian graphical model with couplings only on the true contacts, so right and wrong have exact answers: the exact precision matrix is <b>identically zero</b> at non-contacts (numerically of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>) while the correlation matrix there is of order 0.08 — a dozen orders of magnitude apart. That gap is the entire value of inverting."},{"p":"But drag the “homologous sequences seen” slider to the left and the advantage collapses: with few sequences the sample covariance is itself noisy, and inverting amplifies noise. That is DCA’s real situation for twenty years — it works only for families with very many sequences, and is useless for orphan proteins and for designed new proteins, which have no homologues at all."},{"h":"From a statistical model to a network"},{"p":"In 2019 trRosetta turned this into end-to-end learning: instead of estimating one family’s coupling matrix, train a network to predict distributions of inter-residue distances and orientations from the alignment, then fold with Rosetta <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yang2019\" data-ref=\"yang2019\">Yang 2019</a>]</span>. This is the transitional form between statistics and networks, and AlphaFold2 took the same route, only further."},{"p":"The next step was getting rid of the alignment. Protein language models do masked prediction over hundreds of millions of sequences — hide a residue and have the model guess it — without targeting any one family. In 2021 ESM-1b showed that structural and functional information <b>emerges by itself</b> in such representations <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rives2021\" data-ref=\"rives2021\">Rives 2021</a>]</span>; in 2023 ESM-2 and ESMFold pushed it to structure from a single sequence with no alignment, fast enough to fold hundreds of millions of metagenomic sequences <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lin2023\" data-ref=\"lin2023\">Lin 2023</a>]</span>. Parameter sharing is the key: DCA fits a separate set of parameters per family, while a language model explains every family with one set, so sparse families borrow statistics from dense ones."},{"h":"They generate, not only predict"},{"list":["<b>ESM3 and esmGFP</b> (2025): ESM3 trains on sequence, structure and function tracks at once and can be conditioned on any of them. The fluorescent protein it generated, esmGFP, is only 58 % identical to its closest natural relative (36 % to avGFP) and <b>does fluoresce</b> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hayes2025\" data-ref=\"hayes2025\">Hayes 2025</a>]</span>. The widely repeated “equivalent to 500 million years of evolution” is an estimate fitted to six anthozoan pairs, not a measurement, and the two should not be confused.","<b>ESMC / ESMFold2</b> (2026): from sequence alone, an antibody–antigen DockQ pass rate of 50 ± 2 %, above AlphaFold 3’s 47 ± 2 % with an alignment; the same work includes wet-lab results, with minibinder hit rates of 36–88 % and scFv 15–29 % across five targets and affinities from 68 pM to 70 nM <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-candido2026\" data-ref=\"candido2026\">Candido 2026</a>]</span>. This is the first time the language-model route went head to head with the structure-model route on binders without losing.","<b>ProGen2 → ProGen3</b> (2023, 2025): Profluent trained eight models from 112 M to 46 B parameters and measured, in an expression assay, how many generated sequences are valid: from 4 % for ProGen2-XL to 49 % at 46 B <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-nijkamp2023\" data-ref=\"nijkamp2023\">Nijkamp 2023</a>; <a href=\"#ref-bhatnagar2025\" data-ref=\"bhatnagar2025\">Bhatnagar 2025</a>]</span>. That is rare direct evidence in this field that scale buys hit rate.","<b>xTrimoPGLM, SaProt, Pro-PRIME</b>: three different routes from Chinese groups — a hundred-billion-parameter model that both understands and generates <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chen2025\" data-ref=\"chen2025\">Chen 2025</a>]</span>; folding structure into part of the vocabulary and training it with sequence <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-su2023\" data-ref=\"su2023\">Su 2023</a>]</span>; and temperature-conditioned redesign of enzyme stability and activity with wet-lab validation, with an erratum in 2025 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jiang2024\" data-ref=\"jiang2024\">Jiang 2024</a>]</span>.","<b>One level down: nucleic-acid models.</b> Evo and Evo 2 train directly on DNA, so one model covers genes, RNA and proteins; Evo 2 used nine trillion bases with a million-base context and released both weights and dataset <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-nguyen2024\" data-ref=\"nguyen2024\">Nguyen 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-brixi2026\" data-ref=\"brixi2026\">Brixi 2026</a>]</span>. The price is that it produces no three-dimensional structure, so structure-conditioned design still needs another model."]},{"h":"The most useful and most over-read application: variant effects"},{"p":"Given a sequence, a model can compute the log-likelihood ratio of substituting residue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and rank which substitutions are damaging. AlphaMissense used this idea to call 89 % of all 71 million possible human missense variants <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cheng2023\" data-ref=\"cheng2023\">Cheng 2023</a>]</span>. ProteinGym is the standard benchmark for the task <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-notin2023\" data-ref=\"notin2023\">Notin 2023</a>]</span>."},{"note":"One caution: if homologues of the assayed family sit in the training set, a “zero-shot” score is inflated, because the model has in effect seen relatives. Several 2025–2026 papers are redoing these benchmarks and the answer is not settled (<a href=\"#c-wet\" data-open=\"wet\">10 · How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars</a>). When you see “zero-shot Spearman 0.5”, ask how the training split was made.","kind":"warn"}],"points":[{"h":"Coevolution is a biological fact, not a statistical coincidence","p":"Contacting positions must change together compensatorily, so the contact map really is written into the statistics of homologous sequences <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-marks2011\" data-ref=\"marks2011\">Marks 2011</a>; <a href=\"#ref-morcos2011\" data-ref=\"morcos2011\">Morcos 2011</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"“Invert to remove indirect correlation” is provable","p":"In a Gaussian graphical model the precision matrix is identically zero at non-contacts while the covariance is nonzero everywhere. In the demo these differ by a dozen orders of magnitude, computed exactly.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Finite samples eat the advantage","p":"The covariance is estimated to about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> and inverting amplifies that, so DCA fails on sparse families — which is exactly where protein language models, with their shared parameters, come in.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Language models absorb the statistics into parameters","p":"They never form a coupling matrix, yet structural and functional information emerges in their representations <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rives2021\" data-ref=\"rives2021\">Rives 2021</a>]</span>, which makes structure from a single sequence without an alignment possible <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lin2023\" data-ref=\"lin2023\">Lin 2023</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Scale did buy wet-lab hit rate","p":"ProGen3’s 4 % → 49 % from 112 M to 46 B parameters is the most direct scale-to-hit-rate curve in the public literature <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bhatnagar2025\" data-ref=\"bhatnagar2025\">Bhatnagar 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"“500 million years of evolution” is an estimate, not a measurement","p":"That esmGFP fluoresces is an experimental fact; the time equivalence was fitted to the sequence divergence of six anthozoan pairs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hayes2025\" data-ref=\"hayes2025\">Hayes 2025</a>]</span>. The two are not the same grade of evidence.","st":"disputed"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>Z</mi></mfrac><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Potts model, where DCA starts: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math> for single-site preferences, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></semantics></math> for pairwise couplings. It is the generalised Ising model of statistical physics (<a href=\"../macro/\">Heat, statistics and flow</a>)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟺</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>⊥</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><mtext>the rest</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The exact statement in a Gaussian graphical model: a zero in the precision matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟺</mo><mtext>  </mtext></mrow></semantics></math> conditional independence given the rest. This is the justification for inverting, and what the demo uses."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>APC:</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⋅</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mo>⋅</mo></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The average product correction: some positions are globally more “active” (surface-exposed, say), and this term divides that single-site effect out. It is a standard step in real DCA pipelines."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">L</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">M</mi></mrow></munder><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">M</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mtext>var</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>mut</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>wt</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The masked language-model objective, and the log-likelihood ratio used to score a substitution. The latter is the basic quantity behind AlphaMissense and most methods on ProteinGym <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cheng2023\" data-ref=\"cheng2023\">Cheng 2023</a>; <a href=\"#ref-notin2023\" data-ref=\"notin2023\">Notin 2023</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"Doing DCA on a 100-position family: how many parameters have to be fitted, and how many effective sequences does a typical family have?","steps":["Couplings: one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></semantics></math> matrix per pair of positions (20 amino acids plus a gap symbol), and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0px\"><mn>100</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4950</mn></mrow></semantics></math> pairs.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4950</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>441</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>182</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>950</mn></mrow></semantics></math> coupling parameters plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2100</mn></mrow></semantics></math> single-site terms, about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.19</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> in all.","After removing redundancy, a mid-sized Pfam family typically has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> effective sequences.","That leaves under 0.01 observations per parameter on average."],"a":"About 2.2 million parameters against one to ten thousand sequences. That ratio explains DCA’s whole character: strong regularisation is mandatory (shrinkage, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, mean-field approximations), it works only for very dense families, and it is useless for newly designed proteins, which have no homologues. Protein language models get around this not with “more data” but with <b>parameter sharing</b>: one set of parameters explains every family, so one family’s statistics can be lent to another. That is why they give useful scores for orphan proteins and DCA does not."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-marks2011\" data-ref=\"marks2011\">Marks 2011</a> · <a href=\"#ref-morcos2011\" data-ref=\"morcos2011\">Morcos 2011</a> · <a href=\"#ref-yang2019\" data-ref=\"yang2019\">Yang 2019</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rives2021\" data-ref=\"rives2021\">Rives 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-cheng2023\" data-ref=\"cheng2023\">Cheng 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-lin2023\" data-ref=\"lin2023\">Lin 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-nijkamp2023\" data-ref=\"nijkamp2023\">Nijkamp 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-notin2023\" data-ref=\"notin2023\">Notin 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-su2023\" data-ref=\"su2023\">Su 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-jiang2024\" data-ref=\"jiang2024\">Jiang 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-nguyen2024\" data-ref=\"nguyen2024\">Nguyen 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bhatnagar2025\" data-ref=\"bhatnagar2025\">Bhatnagar 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chen2025\" data-ref=\"chen2025\">Chen 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hayes2025\" data-ref=\"hayes2025\">Hayes 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-brixi2026\" data-ref=\"brixi2026\">Brixi 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-candido2026\" data-ref=\"candido2026\">Candido 2026</a>"},"inverse":{"id":"inverse","body":[{"p":"The task is well defined: the backbone coordinates are given — from a natural structure, from rule-based construction, or from a generative model — and a sequence has to be written that folds into it. Rosetta searched the joint space of sequence and side-chain conformations, which is slow; ProteinMPNN in 2022 turned it into autoregressive decoding on a graph neural network: each residue is a node, edges carry neighbour distances and orientations, and the network writes out amino acids one at a time in some order <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a>]</span>."},{"table":{"head":["","Rosetta","ProteinMPNN"],"rows":[["Native sequence recovery (CATH test set)","32.9 %","52.4 %"],["Time per sequence","Minutes","About 200× faster (sub-second)"],["Ligand-binding scaffolds Rosetta had failed on: AlphaFold self-consistency","2.7 %","54.1 %"]],"cap":"The three numbers reported in the ProteinMPNN paper <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a>]</span>. The last row is the most telling: not “a little better on a benchmark”, but a class of task that did not work before and now works."}},{"h":"Another route the same year: train on predicted structures"},{"p":"ESM-IF1 trained on twelve million <b>predicted</b> structures and showed that predicted structures work as training data <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hsu2022\" data-ref=\"hsu2022\">Hsu 2022</a>]</span>. That became routine: there are a few hundred thousand experimental structures in the PDB and hundreds of millions of predicted ones, and the data bottleneck was simply bypassed. The price is that the predictor’s errors are learned too, so such models are equally unreliable exactly where the predictor is unsure — disordered regions and flexible loops."},{"h":"The 2025 patch: condition on the ligand too"},{"p":"The original ProteinMPNN sees only protein atoms, so it writes the wrong residues near metal sites, cofactor pockets and nucleic-acid interfaces — it does not know anything is there. LigandMPNN conditions on the atoms of ligands, nucleic acids and metals, raising recovery near metal sites from 36–41 % to 77.5 %, with more than 100 designed interfaces binding experimentally <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2025\" data-ref=\"dauparas2025\">Dauparas 2025</a>]</span>. For a chemist this is the most familiar step of all: it puts the first coordination sphere back into the scoring."},{"h":"Why “recovery” is a misleading number"},{"p":"The demo below compresses the environment into a single number (burial) so that the relation between recovery, diversity and temperature can be seen directly."},{"demo":"mpnn"},{"steps":["<b>Lower the temperature, recovery rises, diversity goes to zero.</b> In the limit the model gives one answer per position: recovery is maximal, but what you get is copies of the same sequence, so testing them is testing a single sample.","<b>The native sequence is not the only answer.</b> Recovery measures agreement with the native, yet the same backbone has an astronomical number of valid sequences (see this chapter’s worked example). So 100 % recovery is neither attainable nor desirable.","<b>Recovery does not weight positions by importance.</b> Buried positions admit almost only hydrophobic residues (low entropy, easy to get right) while surface positions are nearly free (high entropy, easy to get wrong). A model that gets every core position right and every surface position wrong can have the same recovery as the reverse, with completely different experimental outcomes."]},{"note":"In practice the standard procedure is therefore not to look at recovery but to <b>sample a batch and filter</b>: draw tens to hundreds of sequences at several temperatures, predict a structure for each, keep those that come back to the target backbone (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>scRMSD</mtext><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å with high pLDDT), and rank the survivors on other criteria. Recovery is used to compare models, never to judge a single design.","kind":"info"},{"p":"Inverse folding is now the most reliable link in the chain: generators from <a href=\"#c-gen\" data-open=\"gen\">06 · Generating backbones: how a diffusion model grows a protein</a> provide a backbone, it writes the sequence, and the predictors of <a href=\"#c-fold\" data-open=\"fold\">02 · Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design</a> referee. One figure OpenAI reported in 2026 confirms this indirectly: rewriting sequences for <b>existing</b> backbones hit 75 of 88 on MDM2, while generating backbones de novo and designing into them hit 35 of 322 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>]</span>. Writing a sequence really has become much easier than generating a backbone."}],"points":[{"h":"Inverse folding is the most mature link in the pipeline","p":"52.4 % recovery, 200× the speed, and self-consistency from 2.7 % to 54.1 % <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a>]</span> — the precondition for the whole pipeline running at all.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Predicted structures work as training data","p":"ESM-IF1, trained on twelve million predicted structures, established this <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hsu2022\" data-ref=\"hsu2022\">Hsu 2022</a>]</span>, at the cost of learning the predictor’s mistakes with it.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The chemical environment has to go into the model","p":"A model blind to ligands writes the wrong residues at metal sites; conditioning on ligand atoms took recovery from 36–41 % to 77.5 % <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2025\" data-ref=\"dauparas2025\">Dauparas 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"High recovery does not mean good design","p":"At low temperature recovery is high and diversity zero, and the native sequence is only one point in the solution set. Designs are judged by the fraction that expresses, folds and functions.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Per-position entropy varies, and the average hides it","p":"Buried positions have low entropy and surface positions high. Real pipelines look at entropy per position — the demo plots it — rather than at one average recovery number.","st":"approx"},{"h":"The standard practice is sample-then-filter","p":"Draw tens to hundreds, discard those that are not self-consistent under structure prediction, then rank the rest. The quality of that filter often decides the outcome more than the quality of the generator (<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>).","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">X</mi><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The autoregressive factorisation: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">X</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the backbone and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the decoding order. ProteinMPNN randomises the order, which is what lets residues be fixed anywhere — redesigning part of a protein while keeping an active site."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>recovery</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mn mathvariant=\"double-struck\">1</mn><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∣</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>native</mtext></msubsup><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>recovery</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>native</mtext></msubsup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The definition of recovery, and the closed form for its expectation under sampling — the demo uses the latter, so no sampling is needed."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Temperature sampling and per-position entropy. As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> entropy vanishes, recovery peaks and diversity is zero; as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow></semantics></math> it tends to the uniform distribution."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>eff</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>H</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>H</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mtext>mean entropy per position, in bits</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"A rough count of how many valid sequences a backbone admits: multiply the effective choices per position. This is what the worked example computes."}],"example":{"q":"For a 100-residue backbone, if an inverse-folding model has a mean entropy of 1.8 bits per position, roughly how many sequences are valid? And does 100 % recovery mean anything?","steps":["The effective number of choices per position is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>1.8</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3.48</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, not 20: the backbone has already ruled most options out.","For the whole chain: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.8</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>180</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.53</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>54</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> sequences.","Against the whole sequence space: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.53</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>54</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.27</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>130</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>76</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","The native sequence is one of those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>54</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Demanding that the model’s maximum-likelihood answer be exactly that one asks it to reproduce one specific answer to a question with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>54</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> valid ones."],"a":"About <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>54</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> valid sequences, a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>76</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> fraction of the whole space. Two conclusions: <b>the backbone is an extremely strong constraint</b> — it compresses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>130</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>54</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, equivalent to cutting each position from 20 choices to 3.5 — and <b>100 % recovery is meaningless</b>, since it asks for one particular answer out of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>54</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> valid ones. The value of 52.4 % is for comparing models, not for its distance from 100 %. One corollary as a check: if a model reports unusually high recovery, say 80 %, suspect homology leakage between test and training sets before concluding that it understands folding better."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-dauparas2022\" data-ref=\"dauparas2022\">Dauparas 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hsu2022\" data-ref=\"hsu2022\">Hsu 2022</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dauparas2025\" data-ref=\"dauparas2025\">Dauparas 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>"},"gen":{"id":"gen","body":[{"p":"In 2021 there was still a stopgap called network hallucination: since a structure-prediction network reports confidence in its own output, optimise the input sequence to maximise that confidence, and what comes out is often a new, self-consistent structure <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-anishchenko2021\" data-ref=\"anishchenko2021\">Anishchenko 2021</a>]</span>. It worked, but it was slow and gave no way to say “I want a scaffold that holds this motif”."},{"h":"Diffusion: run the destruction backwards"},{"p":"A diffusion model takes two sentences. <b>Forward</b>: add a little Gaussian noise to a real structure over and over until after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> steps no information is left and it is pure noise — this step is not learned, it is defined. <b>Backward</b>: train a network that, given a noised structure and the noise level, predicts the clean structure (equivalently, predicts the noise). Then start from pure noise and walk back step by step, and a new structure grows."},{"p":"For proteins there is one extra requirement: <b>equivariance</b>. Rotate and translate the whole structure and the prediction must rotate with it, or the network has to learn every orientation of the same structure separately. FrameDiff treats each residue as a rigid-body frame — a position plus an orientation — and diffuses on the group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yim2023\" data-ref=\"yim2023\">Yim 2023</a>]</span>. This is the same problem as <a href=\"../nequip/\">equivariance in machine-learning potentials</a>, and the mathematics behind it is <a href=\"../group-theory/\">group representation theory</a>."},{"demo":"diff"},{"p":"In the demo a “structure” is a chain of points in the plane and the prior is an elastic network: second differences of three consecutive points must be small, so chains are smooth. The optimal denoiser then has a closed form and reverse diffusion samples the prior <b>exactly</b> — what you see is not an approximation. Two switches are worth playing with:"},{"list":["<b>Use only the posterior mean at every step.</b> The chains come out over-smooth with too little variance — in the demo the trace of the sample covariance is around 40 % of the prior — and that is the mathematical origin of the complaint that diffusion designs look too average. Sample from the posterior at each step instead and the sample covariance returns to the prior (about 0.97), which can be verified exactly.","<b>Fix a motif.</b> Hold a few points still and let the rest grow from the conditional distribution. That is motif scaffolding: pin a known active site, epitope or binding motif and have the model grow a scaffold around it. “Specifying the active site” in a real pipeline is exactly this (<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>)."]},{"h":"RFdiffusion and after"},{"p":"In 2023 RFdiffusion initialised a diffusion model from RoseTTAFold’s weights and solved several tasks at once <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a>]</span>:"},{"table":{"head":["Task","Result"],"rows":[["Motif-scaffolding benchmark (25 problems)","23 solved, against 15 for hallucination and 19 for inpainting"],["Binders (five targets, 95 designs each)","19 % bound experimentally"],["p53-helix-scaffolding binders","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> = 0.5 and 0.7 nM"]],"cap":"Numbers from the RFdiffusion paper. It is open source with released weights, which matters for everything after it: it became the default baseline."}},{"p":"The same year Chroma from Generate Biomedicines took the programmable-conditioning route: symmetry, shape, secondary structure, even a sentence of text can be a condition <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ingraham2023\" data-ref=\"ingraham2023\">Ingraham 2023</a>]</span>. Over the next two years generation became a crowded field: Genie 2 from Columbia <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lin2024\" data-ref=\"lin2024\">Genie 2 2024</a>]</span>, and Proteina from NVIDIA with flow matching plus scale <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-geffner2025\" data-ref=\"geffner2025\">Geffner 2025</a>]</span>, each competing on the same three benchmark quantities — designability, novelty and diversity."},{"note":"<b>These three pull against each other, so any one of them alone means nothing.</b> Designability — whether a self-consistent sequence can be written for the generated backbone — can be inflated by generating the most conventional structures, which kills novelty; novelty, being unlike anything in the PDB, can be inflated by generating oddities, which kills designability. Read all three together, and remember that all of them are computational, none is a wet-lab number.","kind":"warn"},{"h":"In 2026: conditioning at the atom level"},{"p":"RFdiffusion2 specifies an active site <b>atom by atom</b>, with no inverse-rotamer sampling and no residue numbering, and passes 41 of 41 enzyme-scaffolding benchmarks where the original managed 16 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ahern2026\" data-ref=\"ahern2026\">Ahern 2026</a>]</span>; the enzymes it produced are in <a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>. Chinese groups moved in other directions at the same time: Westlake’s Pinal takes an order in one sentence (16 B parameters, 1.7 B protein–text pairs over 135 M proteins, and 5 of 5 tested alcohol-dehydrogenase designs active) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dai2026\" data-ref=\"dai2026\">Dai 2026</a>]</span>; AnewOmni from Tsinghua, ByteDance and others produces peptides, macrocycles, nanobodies and small molecules from one all-atom model, with 7 of 30 linear peptides inhibiting KRAS G12D by more than half <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kong2026\" data-ref=\"kong2026\">Kong 2026</a>]</span>; and CheMoDesign from Peking University designs around non-canonical amino acids and post-translational modifications, reaching a carbonic anhydrase IX inhibitor at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> 45.4 nM <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-li2026\" data-ref=\"li2026\">Li 2026</a>]</span>."},{"p":"There is now a clear division of labour in backbone generation: <b>the generator decides which wishes you can express</b> (symmetry, shape, an atom-level active site, a sentence), and <b>the filter decides your hit rate</b> (<a href=\"#c-wet\" data-open=\"wet\">10 · How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars</a>). That is also why the part that genuinely improved in the 2026 reports is usually the filter."}],"points":[{"h":"The forward process of diffusion is not learned","p":"Adding noise is defined rather than learned, which makes the training objective clean: given the noise level, predict the clean structure or the noise itself.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Equivariance is a requirement, not an optimisation","p":"A non-equivariant network has to learn every orientation of the same structure separately. FrameDiff diffuses on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yim2023\" data-ref=\"yim2023\">Yim 2023</a>]</span>, the same mathematical problem as in <a href=\"../nequip/\">machine-learning potentials</a>.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Using only the posterior mean systematically shrinks variance","p":"With a Gaussian prior this is exact: sampling the posterior each step reproduces the prior covariance, while taking the mean drops it to around 40 %. “Designs look too average” has a precise mathematical source.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Motif scaffolding is conditional sampling","p":"Pin some coordinates and generate the rest from the conditional distribution. RFdiffusion’s 23 of 25 and RFdiffusion2’s 41 of 41 are both this task <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a>; <a href=\"#ref-ahern2026\" data-ref=\"ahern2026\">Ahern 2026</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Designability, novelty and diversity pull against each other","p":"Any one of them can be inflated alone, so all three must be read together — and all three are computational metrics, not experimental success rates.","st":"approx"},{"h":"An open baseline changed the pace of the field","p":"Once RFdiffusion released its weights, every new paper had to compare against it; work that does not release weights, such as AlphaProteo or Chai-2, is correspondingly hard to check independently (<a href=\"#c-binder\" data-open=\"binder\">07 · Binders and antibodies: how to read a hit rate</a>).","st":"history"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">N</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>α</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>α</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">I</mi><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>α</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The closed form of the forward process: any step can be sampled in one shot without simulating the intermediate ones, which is why training is efficient."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">N</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">I</mi><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"One step of the reverse process. <b>That <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math> cannot be dropped</b>: dropping it is the mean-only route, and the variance collapses all the way down."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The definition of equivariance. Energy is invariant (a scalar) while forces and coordinate updates are equivariant (vectors); the distinction is treated at length in <a href=\"../nequip/\">NequIP</a>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mtext>free</mtext></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mtext>motif</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mtext>free</mtext></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mtext>motif</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mtext>motif</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"All there is to motif scaffolding: a conditional distribution. Under a Gaussian prior it stays Gaussian with closed-form mean and covariance, which is what the demo computes."}],"example":{"q":"A one-dimensional example: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and we observe <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. What variance does the output have if you take the posterior mean, and if you sample the posterior?","steps":["The posterior is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">N</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>: both variances are 1, so the weights are one half each and the posterior variance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>.","Mean only: the output is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so the variance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Sampling the posterior: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (from the mean) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (the posterior variance) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, exactly the prior.","Over many steps, dropping the posterior variance each time shrinks the total by a product of such factors, which is where the 0.42 in the demo comes from."],"a":"Mean only gives 0.5, half the prior; sampling the posterior gives 1, <b>exactly</b> the prior. This is why the noise term in the reverse process cannot be skipped: it is not “randomness added for diversity” but the component that makes the generated distribution equal the target distribution. Lose half per step and after twenty steps you are down to order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> — real diffusion models are of course not that extreme, their <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is merely a little small — but the familiar complaint that designs look too average and lack the irregularity of natural proteins has its root right here."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-anishchenko2021\" data-ref=\"anishchenko2021\">Anishchenko 2021</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ingraham2023\" data-ref=\"ingraham2023\">Ingraham 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-yim2023\" data-ref=\"yim2023\">Yim 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-lin2024\" data-ref=\"lin2024\">Genie 2 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-geffner2025\" data-ref=\"geffner2025\">Geffner 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ahern2026\" data-ref=\"ahern2026\">Ahern 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dai2026\" data-ref=\"dai2026\">Dai 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kong2026\" data-ref=\"kong2026\">Kong 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-li2026\" data-ref=\"li2026\">Li 2026</a>"},"binder":{"id":"binder","body":[{"p":"A binder is this field’s most practical product: a small protein that sticks specifically to a chosen target. It can be an inhibitor blocking a receptor, a diagnostic reagent, the targeting head of a drug delivery system, or the scaffold of a vaccine antigen. Designing one de novo needs no animal immunisation and no library, and the molecules are small, express well and are thermally stable."},{"h":"Get the arithmetic straight first"},{"p":"A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> running from micromolar to picomolar spans six orders of magnitude, which sounds far, but in free energy it is only 8 kcal/mol. At room temperature each order of magnitude is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.364</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol — one or two hydrogen bonds. That conversion sets the difficulty of the whole enterprise: you need a scoring function reliable at the 1 kcal/mol scale, and it is not."},{"demo":"kd"},{"h":"What each group reported"},{"table":{"head":["Source","Success rate","Best affinity","Weights"],"rows":[["RFdiffusion (2023, 95 designs each on five targets) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a>]</span>","19 %","0.5 nM (p53 scaffolding)","Open"],["AlphaProteo (2024, seven targets) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a>]</span>","9–88 %, and 0 of 54 on TNFα","82 pM (IL-7Rα)","Not released"],["BindCraft (2025) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pacesa2025\" data-ref=\"pacesa2025\">Pacesa 2025</a>]</span>","10–100 %","Low nanomolar","Open (MIT)"],["Chai-2 (2025, 52 novel targets, ≤ 20 designs each) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a>]</span>","15.5 % average; at least one binder for 26 of 52","Not reported separately","Not released"],["PXDesign (2025, seven targets) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pxdesign2025\" data-ref=\"pxdesign2025\">PXDesign 2025</a>]</span>","17–82 % on six targets; TNF-α still failed","Nanomolar","Not stated"],["RFantibody (2025, about 9,000 designs per target) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bennett2025\" data-ref=\"bennett2025\">Bennett 2025</a>]</span>","The authors call it “still quite low”","78 nM (influenza HA)","Open"],["ESMFold2 (2026, five targets) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-candido2026\" data-ref=\"candido2026\">Candido 2026</a>]</span>","36–88 % minibinders, 15–29 % scFv","68 pM","Open"],["GPT-6 (2026, five targets, 322 designs) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>]</span>","10.9 % (35 of 322)","Not given in the post","Not released"]],"cap":"Numbers in one column are not comparable: different targets, different numbers of designs per target, and different thresholds for “success” (a binding signal for some, sub-nanomolar for others). Read all three together."}},{"h":"Three conclusions that do hold"},{"steps":["<b>The target decides everything.</b> The same method gives 88 % on one target and 0 % on another. TNFα defeated AlphaProteo (0 of 54) and PXDesign independently, with no agreed explanation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-pxdesign2025\" data-ref=\"pxdesign2025\">PXDesign 2025</a>]</span>. “Our success rate is X %” carries no information without naming the target.","<b>Polar targets are hard and hydrophobic interfaces are easy.</b> The Baker lab’s own 2026 review lists polar targets, sugars and disordered regions as the first unsolved problem <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yang2026\" data-ref=\"yang2026\">Yang 2026</a>]</span>. There is a physical reason: hydrophobic burial provides a large and forgiving driving force, while hydrogen-bond networks and desolvation penalties have to be computed accurately.","<b>The quality of the filter often decides the hit rate more than the quality of the generator.</b> In the meta-analysis of 3,766 published designs the best single predictor is AlphaFold 3’s ipSAE, yet the same metric’s precision ranges from 0.1 to 1.0 between targets, and adding more features does not generalise to unseen targets <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a>]</span>. <a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a> writes that arithmetic out."]},{"h":"Antibodies are a separate problem"},{"p":"Antibodies bind through six hypervariable loops (the CDRs), the loops themselves are flexible, and antibody–antigen interfaces are frequently polar — precisely the two difficulties above. In the RFantibody preprint about 9,000 designs per target were screened by yeast display, the authors themselves call the success rate “still quite low”, the best was 78 nM against influenza HA, and of five complexes imaged by cryo-EM four matched the prediction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bennett2025\" data-ref=\"bennett2025\">Bennett 2025</a>]</span>. Chai-2’s zero-shot design against 52 novel targets with only 20 designs each is the other route: a 15.5 % hit rate, but with closed weights, so nobody outside can check it <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Not a laboratory curiosity any more"},{"p":"The first designed protein to become an approved medicine was SKYCovione (GBP510): a vaccine built on a designed icosahedral nanoparticle, with a neutralising titre 2.93× the comparator in interim phase 3 results from 4,036 participants, approved in South Korea in 2022 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gbp510\" data-ref=\"gbp510\">GBP510 2023</a>]</span>. Elsewhere, several 2026 papers from China pushed binders in a more chemical direction: AnewOmni’s nanobodies and macrocycles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kong2026\" data-ref=\"kong2026\">Kong 2026</a>]</span>, and CheMoDesign’s 8.4 nM IL-7Rα binder built with non-canonical amino acids and post-translational modifications <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-li2026\" data-ref=\"li2026\">Li 2026</a>]</span>. By 2026 experimentally validated binders covered more than 200 targets <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yang2026\" data-ref=\"yang2026\">Yang 2026</a>]</span>."}],"points":[{"h":"Six orders of magnitude in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> are only 8 kcal/mol","p":"Each order of magnitude is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.364</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol at 298 K. The entire difficulty of affinity engineering is that scoring has to be reliable at the scale of one or two hydrogen bonds.","st":"proved"},{"h":"A success rate without a target is meaningless","p":"The same method spans 9 %–88 %, and TNFα defeated two groups independently <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-pxdesign2025\" data-ref=\"pxdesign2025\">PXDesign 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Polar targets are the acknowledged first problem","p":"The field’s own review says so <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yang2026\" data-ref=\"yang2026\">Yang 2026</a>]</span>: hydrophobic burial is forgiving, hydrogen bonds and desolvation are not.","st":"open"},{"h":"An open baseline makes numbers checkable","p":"Once BindCraft was released under MIT it became everyone’s control, while AlphaProteo and Chai-2 release no weights, so outsiders can only read the numbers in the papers <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pacesa2025\" data-ref=\"pacesa2025\">Pacesa 2025</a>; <a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"history"},{"h":"Antibodies are harder than minibinders, because of flexibility and polarity","p":"RFantibody screened about 9,000 designs per target and the authors still call the success rate low <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bennett2025\" data-ref=\"bennett2025\">Bennett 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"One designed protein is an approved medicine","p":"The nanoparticle antigen of SKYCovione, approved in South Korea in 2022 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gbp510\" data-ref=\"gbp510\">GBP510 2023</a>]</span>. That is the empirical answer to whether any of this reaches the clinic.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Affinity and free energy. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is referenced to 1 M, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nM corresponds to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>12.3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol at 298 K."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.364</mn><mtext> kcal/mol </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>298.15</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The price of one order of magnitude. The slider in the demo moves along this straight line."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Occupancy: exactly one half when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. This is why a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> an order of magnitude below the physiological concentration is what matters pharmacologically."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>at least one hit</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The chance of at least one hit from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> designs each independently hitting with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The worked example uses it to test what “average hit rate” can mean."}],"example":{"q":"Chai-2 reports 52 novel targets, at most 20 designs each, a 15.5 % average hit rate — and binders for only 26 of the 52. Are those numbers consistent?","steps":["First assume hits are independent with the same 15.5 % probability on every target: the chance of at least one is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.155</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>20</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.84</mn><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>20</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>0.845</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.1684</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, times 20 gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.369</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.369</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0344</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. So 96.6 % of targets should yield at least one hit — 50 of the 52.","Only 26 did. The assumption of a uniform per-target probability is therefore refuted by the data.","Turning it around: at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>52</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1040</mn></mrow></semantics></math> designs, about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.155</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1040</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>161</mn></mrow></semantics></math> hits, all on 26 targets, is an average of 6.2 hits per successful target — a rate of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>31</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math> on those."],"a":"They are consistent, but only in one way: the hit rate is <b>bimodal</b> across targets — about 31 % on half of them and near zero on the rest. That is the most important conclusion of this chapter, and “15.5 % on average” hides it. The same test can be run on any binder report: from the quoted average hit rate and the designs per target, compute how many targets should have succeeded and compare with how many did. A large gap means the method is uneven across targets, and that unevenness is the part that is genuinely unsolved."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-gbp510\" data-ref=\"gbp510\">GBP510 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-watson2023\" data-ref=\"watson2023\">Watson 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bennett2025\" data-ref=\"bennett2025\">Bennett 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-pacesa2025\" data-ref=\"pacesa2025\">Pacesa 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-pxdesign2025\" data-ref=\"pxdesign2025\">PXDesign 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-candido2026\" data-ref=\"candido2026\">Candido 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-didi2026\" data-ref=\"didi2026\">Didi 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kong2026\" data-ref=\"kong2026\">Kong 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-li2026\" data-ref=\"li2026\">Li 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a> · <a href=\"#ref-yang2026\" data-ref=\"yang2026\">Yang 2026</a>"},"enzyme":{"id":"enzyme","body":[{"p":"What an enzyme does can be put in one sentence: <b>it does not change the equilibrium, it lowers the free energy of the transition state</b>. Eyring’s transition-state theory makes that an exponential relation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a>]</span>: every 1.364 kcal/mol off the activation free energy is a tenfold rate increase. That is good news — stabilise the transition state by 10 kcal/mol and you have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>-fold catalysis — and bad news: be wrong by 2 kcal/mol and you are off by thirty."},{"demo":"kcat"},{"h":"Why it was stuck for twenty years"},{"p":"The Kemp eliminases and retro-aldolases designed in 2008 were active but four to six orders of magnitude slower than natural enzymes (<a href=\"#c-rosetta\" data-open=\"rosetta\">03 · Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved</a>). The reasons are layered:"},{"list":["<b>Geometric precision.</b> Catalytic groups must be positioned and oriented to within a few tenths of an ångström; let the backbone relax a little and the transition-state stabilisation is gone.","<b>The whole pathway, not one state.</b> A real catalytic cycle has binding, the chemical step and product release, usually with proton transfers and water coming and going. Optimising one transition state makes another step the bottleneck — exactly what the <a href=\"../reaction/#c-span\">energy-span model</a> is about.","<b>Scoring functions are not reliable at this scale.</b> One to two kcal/mol is the error of implicit solvation models and electrostatic terms.","<b>Dynamics and flexibility.</b> Many natural enzymes clamp the substrate or exclude water through conformational change, which a static design cannot capture."]},{"h":"What happened in 2025–2026"},{"table":{"head":["Result","Hit rate","Best <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> or acceleration"],"rows":[["Serine hydrolases (2025) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lauko2025\" data-ref=\"lauko2025\">Lauko 2025</a>]</span>","1.6 % (2 of 129) to 73 % (8 of 11), depending on how hard the filtering was","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> M⁻¹s⁻¹, about 60,000× earlier designed enzymes"],["Metallohydrolases (2025) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kim2025\" data-ref=\"kim2025\">Kim 2025</a>]</span>","5 of 96 in the first round, 11 in the second","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> M⁻¹s⁻¹ where earlier designed metalloenzymes managed 3–60; the crystal structure sits 0.8 Å from the design"],["Metalloproteases (2026) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chen2026\" data-ref=\"chen2026\">Chen 2026</a>]</span>","36 % of 135 cut at the intended bond (15 % for zinc only, 88 % for zinc plus water)","Acceleration above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, and above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> after four substitutions; still <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> short of the most efficient natural proteases"],["AlphaProtein Novo (2026) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wu2026\" data-ref=\"wu2026\">Wu 2026</a>]</span>","Kemp eliminase 3 % → 34 % over 1,536 novel-scaffold designs; esterase 0 % → 44 % over 1,569","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> M⁻¹s⁻¹; a nitrene transferase with 99:1 six-ring selectivity and 94 % ee"]],"cap":"All four carry wet-lab data and two have crystal structures matching the design. Note the first row: the same designs, filtering from loose to strict, and a hit rate from 1.6 % to 73 %."}},{"p":"Two things matured at once. First, <b>atom-level conditioning</b>: RFdiffusion2 specifies an active site atom by atom with no rotamer enumeration and no residue numbering, and passes 41 of 41 enzyme-scaffolding benchmarks where the original passed 16 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ahern2026\" data-ref=\"ahern2026\">Ahern 2026</a>]</span>. Second, <b>filtering</b>: the serine-hydrolase paper stacked its sieves — structural self-consistency, active-site geometry, docking against a transition-state analogue — and each layer jumped the hit rate a level <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lauko2025\" data-ref=\"lauko2025\">Lauko 2025</a>]</span>."},{"note":"The single result most worth a chemist’s attention is AlphaProtein Novo’s nitrene transferase: 99:1 six-ring selectivity and 94 % enantiomeric excess, a selectivity <b>no natural enzyme provides</b> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wu2026\" data-ref=\"wu2026\">Wu 2026</a>]</span>. At that point designed enzymes stop being reproductions of natural ones and start being reactions and selectivities evolution never explored — which is exactly what synthetic chemistry wants from them.","kind":"ok"},{"h":"How far is left"},{"p":"Translate the numbers into energy and it becomes clear. The best designed enzymes are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> below the most efficient natural ones, which is 4.1–5.5 kcal/mol — two to four hydrogen bonds. That sounds close, but it is exactly the error scale of the scoring functions, so the remaining gap will not be closed by computing a little more; it needs better filtering, experiments included, and treatment of the whole pathway."},{"p":"The reaction types that work also remain biased towards hydrolyses and eliminations with lower barriers. Carbon–carbon bond formation and C–H activation, the hard reactions synthetic chemistry most wants, have no published de novo success."}],"points":[{"h":"The whole arithmetic of catalysis is one exponential","p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: 1.364 kcal/mol per tenfold <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a>]</span>, so a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> acceleration is 13.6 kcal/mol.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Designed enzymes were stuck on the energy scale for twenty years","p":"The 1–2 kcal/mol requirement is exactly the error of implicit solvation and electrostatics, so activity could only be found by screening.","st":"tested"},{"h":"2025 was the first time a design entered the natural range","p":"The serine hydrolases’ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> M⁻¹s⁻¹ is about 60,000× earlier designs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lauko2025\" data-ref=\"lauko2025\">Lauko 2025</a>]</span>, with crystal structures to support it.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The hit rate is mostly a function of the filtering","p":"The same designs went from 1.6 % to 73 % purely by filtering <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lauko2025\" data-ref=\"lauko2025\">Lauko 2025</a>]</span>. This is the Bayesian arithmetic of <a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Designed enzymes can now reach selectivities nature does not offer","p":"The nitrene transferase’s 99:1 six-ring selectivity and 94 % ee <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wu2026\" data-ref=\"wu2026\">Wu 2026</a>]</span> — the first clear case of going beyond nature.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"Hard reactions have no de novo success yet","p":"Carbon–carbon bond formation, C–H activation and other high-barrier reactions remain outside the published record.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>6.21</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>298</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Eyring equation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a>]</span>. The prefactor is how many attempts per second, so once the activation free energy is fixed, so is the rate."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">‡</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">‡</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.364</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The rate ratio and the difference in activation free energy; the two curves in the demo are this expression."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"2.4em\" maxsize=\"2.4em\">∣</mo><mtext>diffusion limit</mtext></msub><mo>≲</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mtext>–</mtext><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>9</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The ceiling on catalytic efficiency is set by diffusion of the substrate to the active site, so even a perfect enzyme has a limit. Designed enzymes are now at the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> level."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>uncat</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow><mtext>bind</mtext></msubsup><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mtext>bind</mtext></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Pauling’s insight as an equation: catalysis is how much more tightly the transition state binds than the substrate. It is the reason active sites are designed around transition-state analogues."}],"example":{"q":"The designed metalloproteases of 2026 accelerate hydrolysis more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>-fold, while the most efficient natural proteases are a further <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> above that. How many kcal/mol is the gap, and how many hydrogen bonds?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.987</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>298.15</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5925</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.364</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>-fold: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.364</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4.09</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>-fold: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.364</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5.46</mn></mrow></semantics></math> kcal/mol.","A hydrogen bond in a protein is worth roughly 1–2 kcal/mol, so this is two to four hydrogen bonds.","For comparison: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> already achieved is 10.9 kcal/mol, and the four substitutions that added two more orders of magnitude were worth 2.7 kcal/mol."],"a":"4.1–5.5 kcal/mol, two to four hydrogen bonds. The number cuts both ways. Optimistically, the remaining gap is chemically small — a few precisely placed interactions suffice, and the four substitutions that bought two orders of magnitude (2.7 kcal/mol) in 2026 demonstrate it. Pessimistically, 4–5 kcal/mol is also exactly the error range of the scoring functions, so it <b>cannot be computed</b>; it has to be found by filtering and experiment. That is why the 2025–2026 progress came more from filtering harder (1.6 % → 73 %) than from computing better."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-eyring1935\" data-ref=\"eyring1935\">Eyring 1935</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kim2025\" data-ref=\"kim2025\">Kim 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-lauko2025\" data-ref=\"lauko2025\">Lauko 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ahern2026\" data-ref=\"ahern2026\">Ahern 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chen2026\" data-ref=\"chen2026\">Chen 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wu2026\" data-ref=\"wu2026\">Wu 2026</a>"},"general":{"id":"general","body":[{"p":"The first eight chapters are about specialised models, trained separately for structure, for sequence, for backbones. From 2025 another route appeared: have a general large language model take part in protein design directly. The topic runs hot, while the public evidence falls into three very different grades, and telling them apart matters more than remembering the numbers."},{"h":"The first: GPT-4b micro and two reprogramming factors (2025)"},{"p":"The press reported it in January 2025 and OpenAI published its own page on 22 August 2025 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2025retro\" data-ref=\"openai2025retro\">OpenAI 2025 (company post)</a>]</span>. According to that page, GPT-4b micro is “a miniature version of GPT-4o specialized for protein engineering”, initialised from a scaled-down GPT-4o, trained mostly on protein sequences plus biological text and tokenised three-dimensional structure, with prompts up to 64,000 tokens, producing protein sequences including intrinsically disordered regions, and “developed for research purposes and not broadly available”. It redesigned SOX2 (317 residues) and KLF4 (513) of the OSKM set, giving RetroSOX and RetroKLF."},{"table":{"head":["The number in the company post","Independently checkable?"],"rows":[["More than 50-fold higher expression of reprogramming markers than wild-type controls","No: no paper, no data, no sequences"],["Over 30 % of RetroSOX suggestions beat wild-type SOX2; variants differ from wild type by more than 100 amino acids on average","No"],["RetroKLF’s hit rate is “nearly 50 %” against 1 of 19 (about 5 %) for earlier expert-guided KLF4 substitutions","No"],["Over 30 % of mesenchymal stem cells from donors above 50 were SSEA4/TRA-1-60 positive by day 7","No"]],"cap":"As of November 2026 a full-text search of OpenAlex finds no scholarly record of RetroSOX or RetroKLF, and OpenAI’s page links no manuscript and does not say one is under review. These numbers can therefore only be read as company statements: possibly true, currently unverifiable, and reproducible by nobody."}},{"h":"The second: GPT-6’s binders (24 November 2026)"},{"p":"This time there are wet-lab results and the numbers are detailed. From OpenAI’s post, which describes a preprint hosted on its own servers with no DOI, no preprint-server identifier and no named authors, with Onava as the laboratory partner <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>; <a href=\"#ref-gpt6preprint\" data-ref=\"gpt6preprint\">GPT-6 preprint 2026</a>]</span>:"},{"list":["<b>De novo minibinders: 35 of 322 bound (10.9 %)</b> — PD-L1 5, IL-7Rα 3, TrkA 1, VEGF-A 8, IL-17A 18. The post itself states that this is <b>below</b> the hit rates reported in the original RFdiffusion study.","<b>Rewriting sequences for existing backbones does far better</b>: MDM2 75 of 88 (85 %), VEGF-A 26 of 42 (62 %), PD-1 8 of 59 (14 %), and 14 of 107 (13.1 %) for unpublished shapes. This matches the conclusion of <a href=\"#c-inverse\" data-open=\"inverse\">05 · Inverse folding: writing a sequence for a backbone</a>: writing a sequence is easier than generating a backbone.","<b>Antibody-loop redesign</b>: 128 of 182 (70 %) for VHH/IL-4Rα and 179 of 272 (66 %) for therapeutic antibody fragments.","<b>Benchmarks</b>: MotifBench 43.7 % for an internal model against RFdiffusion’s 27.8 %; CATH fold design 18.9 % against Proteina’s 17.1 %; PoseBusters docking 93 % (GPT-6.2) and 96 % (internal) against DiffDock-L’s 50 %; antibody–target DockQ 0.57 and 0.81 against GPT-5.6’s 0.05.","<b>A clear weakness</b>: whole-protein structure prediction reaches a TM-score of only 0.26 and 0.38, against 0.93 for ESMFold2. It designs without folding."]},{"note":"Two items in the post’s own limitations section are worth reading verbatim. First, <b>filtering still relied on specialised models</b>: candidates were filtered with Boltz-2, OpenDDE and ESMFold2, so “without a specialised design model” refers to the generative step and the pipeline end to end is not zero-shot. Second, it warns that on some benchmarks “unusually high accuracy may partly reflect memorization or patterns left by the process used to generate those structures”. A company writing those two sentences down is more informative than any percentage it reports.","kind":"warn"},{"h":"So can a general model design proteins?"},{"p":"Lay the Bayesian arithmetic out and it becomes clear. The demo below splits a hit rate into three things: the prior (the chance a design works at all), the resolving power of the filter, and where the threshold sits."},{"demo":"screen"},{"p":"The conclusion can be stated exactly: a 1 % prior, a filter at AUC 0.92 and keeping the top 7 % of scores gives a hit rate of 9.4 %, an enrichment of 9.4×. In other words, <b>a hit rate around 10 % can be produced entirely by a generator with a 1 % base rate plus a decent sieve</b>, with no understanding of proteins in the generator at all. There is only one way to distinguish the two: publish the unfiltered hit rate, or publish the resolving power of the filter. No company currently does either."},{"h":"Other general routes"},{"steps":["<b>Order it in a sentence.</b> Westlake’s Pinal takes natural language as input (16 B parameters, 1.7 B protein–text pairs over 135 M proteins): in the wet lab 5 of 5 alcohol-dehydrogenase designs were active and 5 of 20 H-proteins, while GFP gave 1 of 29 tested out of 50,000 generated and a PETase 1 of 94 out of 200,000 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dai2026\" data-ref=\"dai2026\">Dai 2026</a>]</span>. The same model spanning two orders of magnitude across tasks is itself important information.","<b>Be general one level down: nucleic-acid models.</b> Evo 2 trained on nine trillion bases with a million-base context and released both weights and dataset <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brixi2026\" data-ref=\"brixi2026\">Brixi 2026</a>]</span>. Its generality is real; the price is that it produces no three-dimensional structure.","<b>A specialised model with a public product: another kind of checkable.</b> Profluent’s OpenCRISPR-1 model is closed, but the designed protein sequence was released free of charge: 403 amino acids from SpCas9 (71.7 % identity), with median editing of 55.7 % against 48.3 % in human cells and median off-target 0.32 % against 6.1 % <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ruffolo2025\" data-ref=\"ruffolo2025\">Ruffolo 2025</a>]</span>. Releasing the sequence lets any laboratory repeat the work, which answers checkability more directly than releasing weights."]},{"p":"One sentence for the state of play in 2026: general models really can take part in design, and on constrained tasks such as writing a sequence for a given backbone or redesigning antibody loops the numbers look good; but they are behind specialised models at generating backbones de novo, far behind at folding, and how much of their hit rate comes from the specialised sieve cannot be separated out from outside. None of which says the results are not real — it says the <b>grade of evidence is below peer-reviewed work</b>, and should be labelled as such. In the results table on the front page these two entries are marked “statement only” and “wet lab, self-published”."}],"points":[{"h":"The 2025 reprogramming results still have no paper","p":"A full-text search of OpenAlex finds no RetroSOX or RetroKLF, the company page links no manuscript, and no sequences were released <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2025retro\" data-ref=\"openai2025retro\">OpenAI 2025 (company post)</a>]</span>. Grade of evidence: statement only.","st":"disputed"},{"h":"The 2026 GPT-6 results have wet-lab data, self-published","p":"The 35 of 322 (10.9 %) and the rest come from a company post and a self-hosted PDF with no DOI and no named authors <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>; <a href=\"#ref-gpt6preprint\" data-ref=\"gpt6preprint\">GPT-6 preprint 2026</a>]</span>.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"“Without specialised models” refers to the generative step only","p":"Filtering used Boltz-2, OpenDDE and ESMFold2, as the post itself says. The pipeline end to end is not zero-shot.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"The stronger the constraint, the better a general model does","p":"Rewriting sequences for an existing backbone gave 75 of 88 on MDM2 while de novo design gave 35 of 322, matching the conclusion of <a href=\"#c-inverse\" data-open=\"inverse\">05 · Inverse folding: writing a sequence for a backbone</a>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"A 10 % hit rate does not show that the generator understands proteins","p":"A 1 % prior plus a sieve at AUC 0.92 keeping the top 7 % already gives 9.4 %. Distinguishing the cases requires publishing the unfiltered hit rate or the sieve’s resolving power.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Releasing sequences is a more direct form of checkability than releasing weights","p":"The OpenCRISPR-1 model is closed and its sequence open, so any laboratory can repeat its editing and off-target measurements <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ruffolo2025\" data-ref=\"ruffolo2025\">Ruffolo 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>hit rate</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Bayes’ rule as the positive predictive value, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the prior hit rate. When reading any design report, this is the only bridge from a hit rate to how good the model is."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>d</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>d</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The equal-variance normal signal-detection model: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.92</mn><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.99</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. This is the conversion the demo uses."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>enrichment</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>hit rate</mtext><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><munderover><mo stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"3.0em\">→</mo><mpadded width=\"+0.6em\" lspace=\"0.3em\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mpadded><mpadded width=\"+0.6em\" lspace=\"0.3em\"><mrow></mrow></mpadded></munderover><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"For a small prior the enrichment tends to the likelihood ratio, independent of the generator. That is the precise form of “the sieve decides the hit rate”."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>95</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>hit rate</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"How many designs are needed for a 95 % chance of at least one hit: 29 at a 10 % hit rate and 299 at 1 %."}],"example":{"q":"GPT-6 reports a de novo hit rate of 10.9 % on candidates filtered with Boltz-2, OpenDDE and ESMFold2. If that sieve has an AUC of 0.92 and keeps the top 7 % of scores, what was the hit rate before filtering?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.92</mn><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.92</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1.99</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. With the threshold at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, the sensitivity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.687</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and the false-positive rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0668</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, keeping 7.0 % overall as stated.","Invert Bayes’ rule: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mtext>hit rate</mtext><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>hit rate</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mtext>hit rate</mtext><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></semantics></math>.","Substituting: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.109</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.0668</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0.687</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.891</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.109</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.0668</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>0.00728</mn><mn>0.6194</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0118</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","That is about 1.2 %, an enrichment of roughly 9.2×."],"a":"About <b>1.2 %</b> — the sieve supplied more than ninefold of enrichment. Note that this is <b>an inference with assumptions</b>: we chose the sieve’s AUC and threshold because the post does not give them, so it must not be quoted as OpenAI’s number. Its use is to make one point: <b>without knowing how strong the sieve is, a 10 % hit rate cannot distinguish a good generator from a mediocre generator with a decent sieve.</b> That is the question to ask of this kind of release, and not only of OpenAI: the serine-hydrolase paper in <a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a> filtered the same designs from 1.6 % to 73 %, and the meta-analysis of 3,766 designs in <a href=\"#c-binder\" data-open=\"binder\">07 · Binders and antibodies: how to read a hit rate</a> found filter precision ranging from 0.1 to 1.0 between targets. There is one way to make the numbers comparable: publish the unfiltered hit rate."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-openai2025retro\" data-ref=\"openai2025retro\">OpenAI 2025 (company post)</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ruffolo2025\" data-ref=\"ruffolo2025\">Ruffolo 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-brixi2026\" data-ref=\"brixi2026\">Brixi 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dai2026\" data-ref=\"dai2026\">Dai 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gpt6preprint\" data-ref=\"gpt6preprint\">GPT-6 preprint 2026</a> · <a href=\"#ref-openai2026gpt6\" data-ref=\"openai2026gpt6\">OpenAI 2026 (company post)</a>"},"wet":{"id":"wet","body":[{"p":"This is the reader’s toolkit chapter. The numbers of the previous nine chapters are on the table; here is how to judge them: grades of evidence, denominators, error bars, and one control group that is routinely forgotten — directed evolution."},{"h":"Six steps of evidence"},{"steps":["<b>A benchmark score</b> (weakest). Designability, novelty, DockQ, TM-score. All computational, and the benchmark may leak homology.","<b>Expression and folding.</b> The protein can be made, is monomeric, shows secondary structure by circular dichroism, and has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> from differential scanning calorimetry. This step removes a great many designs that were beautiful on a computer.","<b>Binding or activity.</b> Surface plasmon resonance or biolayer interferometry for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and kinetics; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> for enzymes. Negative controls and a concentration series are required; a single-point signal is not a measurement.","<b>A structure.</b> A crystal or cryo-EM structure showing that it binds as designed. This is the hardest grade: the metallohydrolase of <a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a> sits 0.8 Å from its design <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kim2025\" data-ref=\"kim2025\">Kim 2025</a>]</span>, and four of five RFantibody complexes in <a href=\"#c-binder\" data-open=\"binder\">07 · Binders and antibodies: how to read a hit rate</a> matched the prediction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bennett2025\" data-ref=\"bennett2025\">Bennett 2025</a>]</span>.","<b>Works in cells or in vivo.</b> A physiological setting, with a dose response.","<b>Independent replication</b> (strongest). Another laboratory repeats it from published sequences or weights. This grade is very rare in this field, because much of the work releases no weights and some releases no sequences."]},{"h":"Denominators, filtering, error bars"},{"p":"“A 73 % hit rate” and “a 1.6 % hit rate” can come from the same batch of designs, differing only in the sieve (<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>). A hit rate therefore has to be reported with three things: <b>how many were made</b> (the denominator), <b>how they were filtered</b> (how many were discarded), and <b>which target</b>. Without one of them the number cannot be compared with anyone else’s."},{"p":"Look at the error bars too. Percentages with small denominators are very unstable: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>73</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math> has a 95 % confidence interval of 43 %–90 %, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>129</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math> gives 0.4 %–5.5 % (this chapter’s worked example computes both). The intervals do not overlap, so the improvement is real; but “73 %” itself cannot be used as a precise number."},{"h":"Three independent reality checks"},{"list":["<b>The CASP16 oligomer assessment</b> (40 targets, 13,630 models): about half are still wrong (DockQ &lt; 0.5), the gain over the baseline is not significant (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.184</mn></mrow></semantics></math>), and on antibody and nanobody targets the baseline’s mean DockQ fell from 0.21 to 0.05 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-casp16cplx2025\" data-ref=\"casp16cplx2025\">CASP16 complexes 2025</a>]</span>. A blind assessment and a self-reported benchmark are not the same thing.","<b>The meta-analysis of 3,766 published binder designs</b>: only 436 (11.6 %) actually bound; the best single metric is AlphaFold 3’s ipSAE, yet its precision ranges from 0.1 to 1.0 between targets and adding features does not generalise to unseen targets <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a>]</span>.","<b>Homology leakage.</b> A “zero-shot” variant-effect score is inflated if relatives of the assayed family sit in the training set. ProteinGym is the standard benchmark <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-notin2023\" data-ref=\"notin2023\">Notin 2023</a>]</span>, and several 2025–2026 papers are redoing its splits with no settled answer yet. When you see a zero-shot score, ask how the training split was made."]},{"h":"The forgotten control: directed evolution"},{"p":"Before asking how impressive AI design is, ask what the old method achieves on the same experimental budget. Directed evolution — build a mutant library, screen it, make the winners the start of the next round — turned enzyme engineering into routine work in the 1990s and won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2018 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-arnold2018\" data-ref=\"arnold2018\">Arnold 2018</a>]</span>; and putting machine learning into that loop is not a new idea either, having been reviewed systematically in 2009 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-romero2009\" data-ref=\"romero2009\">Romero &amp; Arnold 2009</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"evo"},{"p":"The demo compares three strategies on a Kauffman NK fitness landscape: single-step hill climbing (directed evolution), random search, and building a surrogate model and choosing by it <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kauffman1989\" data-ref=\"kauffman1989\">Kauffman &amp; Weinberger 1989</a>]</span>. A few conclusions are robust over the parameters:"},{"list":["When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is small (weak epistasis) hill climbing almost always finds the optimum, and a model adds little, because the problem was easy.","As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></semantics></math> grows, local maxima multiply (the demo counts them), hill climbing starts to get stuck, and model guidance begins to pay.","Push <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></semantics></math> further and the surrogate itself cannot learn, and all three degrade together — <b>a model has no magic when there is not enough data</b> — the same conclusion as <a href=\"../chem-ml/#c-bo\">Bayesian optimisation in chemical machine learning</a>."]},{"note":"A real comparison would carry a lower grade of evidence than this demo: NK is a toy model and the epistasis of real proteins is structured differently. One thing agrees on both sides and is seen over and over in real experiments: <b>stabilising mutations add up to less than their sum</b> (the same slider appears in the demo of <a href=\"#c-rosetta\" data-open=\"rosetta\">03 · Twenty years of physics-based scoring: what Rosetta achieved</a>). Any plan to add ten mutations worth 1 kcal/mol each and collect 10 kcal/mol needs that discount first.","kind":"info"},{"h":"A checklist for the reader"},{"table":{"head":["Ask this","Why","Where it came up"],"rows":[["What is the denominator?","Percentages on small denominators have enormous error bars (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>11</mn></mrow></semantics></math> spans 43 %–90 %).","<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>"],["How much was filtered out?","The same designs can go from 1.6 % to 73 % purely by filtering.","<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>"],["Which target, and how many?","One method spans 0 %–88 % across targets, and an average hides a bimodal distribution.","<a href=\"#c-binder\" data-open=\"binder\">07 · Binders and antibodies: how to read a hit rate</a>"],["Is there a structure?","A binding signal can come from aggregation or non-specific effects; only a crystal or cryo-EM structure shows it binds as designed.","<a href=\"#c-enzyme\" data-open=\"enzyme\">08 · Enzymes: from a hundred-thousandfold too slow to inside the natural range</a>"],["Are the weights or sequences public?","Without them nobody can repeat the work, so the evidence stops at “as reported by the authors”.","<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>"],["Was it blind?","In a blind assessment such as CASP, self-reported gains often vanish (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.184</mn></mrow></semantics></math>).","<a href=\"#c-fold\" data-open=\"fold\">02 · Folding: solved enough to referee, not to design</a>"]],"cap":"These six questions are not aimed at anyone in particular. Applied to any row of results in this section, they show how far that number can be taken."}}],"points":[{"h":"Grading evidence matters more than remembering numbers","p":"Benchmark score, expression and folding, binding or activity, a structure, in vivo, independent replication: six grades, each harder than the last. Every result in this section carries one.","st":"history"},{"h":"A percentage on a small denominator is not a precise number","p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>73</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math> has a 95 % interval of 43 %–90 %. Reports should give intervals and rarely do.","st":"proved"},{"h":"A blind assessment is not a self-reported benchmark","p":"In the CASP16 oligomer assessment the gain was not significant (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.184</mn></mrow></semantics></math>), and on antibody targets the baseline went backwards <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-casp16cplx2025\" data-ref=\"casp16cplx2025\">CASP16 complexes 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Only about 11.6 % of published designs actually bound","p":"A meta-analysis over 3,766 designs and 15 targets, with filter precision from 0.1 to 1.0 between targets <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Homology leakage inflates “zero-shot”","p":"Scores rise when relatives of the assayed family are in the training set; several 2025–2026 papers are redoing the splits and the answer is open <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-notin2023\" data-ref=\"notin2023\">Notin 2023</a>]</span>.","st":"disputed"},{"h":"Directed evolution is a control that has to be kept","p":"It made enzyme engineering routine in the 1990s <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-arnold2018\" data-ref=\"arnold2018\">Arnold 2018</a>]</span>, and where epistasis is weak it does as well as a model, without needing one.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Stabilising mutations always add up to less than their sum","p":"Epistasis is among the most reliable regularities in real experiments, and any plan that adds effects linearly needs that discount first.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>±</mo><mi>z</mi><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mtext>(not valid for small </mtext><mi>n</mi><mtext>)</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>Wilson: </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mi>z</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"A confidence interval for a proportion. With small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></semantics></math> near 0 or 1 the normal approximation gives a negative lower bound, so the Wilson interval is used instead — as in the worked example."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Kauffman’s NK landscape <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kauffman1989\" data-ref=\"kauffman1989\">Kauffman &amp; Weinberger 1989</a>]</span>: each position’s contribution depends on itself and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></semantics></math> others. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> it is additive and hill climbing always wins; for large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the number of local maxima grows exponentially."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The crudest model of epistasis: a discount on stacking. Real epistasis can cancel or act synergistically, but sub-linear is the norm."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>enrichment</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>hit rate after filtering</mtext><mtext>hit rate before filtering</mtext></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Quote an enrichment factor alongside a hit rate and others can separate the generator from the sieve (<a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>)."}],"example":{"q":"The serine-hydrolase paper reports hit rates of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>129</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>11</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. What are their 95 % confidence intervals, and does the improvement hold up?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>129</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.55</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Wilson interval at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.96</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.0155</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.0149</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.0298</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0295</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and half-width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.903</mn><mo>×</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.0001183</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.0000577</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0253</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","So the interval is <b>0.4 %–5.5 %</b>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>72.7</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.727</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.175</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.349</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.668</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, half-width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.453</mn><mo>×</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.01804</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.00794</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.234</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","So the interval is <b>43 %–90 %</b>. The two are far apart and do not overlap."],"a":"The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math> spans 0.4 %–5.5 % and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>73</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math> spans 43 %–90 %. <b>The improvement holds up</b> — the intervals do not overlap and differ by more than an order of magnitude — but the figure 73 % itself carries ±25 percentage points of uncertainty, because the denominator is 11. Both things have to be said: the direction is real, the precision is not. For comparison, GPT-6’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>35</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>322</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is 10.9 % with an interval of 7.9 %–14.7 %: a large denominator makes that number more reliable, and its problems lie elsewhere (see <a href=\"#c-general\" data-open=\"general\">09 · General models arrive: what OpenAI did, and how hard the evidence is</a>)."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-kauffman1989\" data-ref=\"kauffman1989\">Kauffman &amp; Weinberger 1989</a> · <a href=\"#ref-romero2009\" data-ref=\"romero2009\">Romero &amp; Arnold 2009</a> · <a href=\"#ref-arnold2018\" data-ref=\"arnold2018\">Arnold 2018</a> · <a href=\"#ref-notin2023\" data-ref=\"notin2023\">Notin 2023</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bennett2025\" data-ref=\"bennett2025\">Bennett 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-casp16cplx2025\" data-ref=\"casp16cplx2025\">CASP16 complexes 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-filterbench2025\" data-ref=\"filterbench2025\">Overath 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kim2025\" data-ref=\"kim2025\">Kim 2025</a>"},"safe":{"id":"safe","body":[{"note":"<b>This chapter covers defensive mechanisms and governance only.</b> It involves no toxin, pathogen or harmful protein, gives no laboratory procedure, and discusses no way of getting around screening. The demo below is pure arithmetic — multiplying probabilities — and involves no sequence of any kind.","kind":"warn"},{"p":"The capability is neutral: the same methods that let someone design a small protein binding a cancer target could in principle be pointed elsewhere. How this field has handled that has one feature worth recording — <b>defensive work and public governance mechanisms developed alongside the capability</b> rather than after an incident. This chapter is about how that machinery works."},{"h":"Where the gates are"},{"p":"The real gates are not at the design step but at <b>synthesis</b>: designing a sequence requires no permission, but turning it into an actual molecule almost always means ordering synthetic DNA from a provider. The International Gene Synthesis Consortium’s harmonised protocol therefore does two things at once: it checks the <b>customer</b> (institution, intended use, bona fides) and the <b>sequence</b> (against controlled lists), escalating flagged orders to human experts, keeping records and referring cases on <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-igsc2024\" data-ref=\"igsc2024\">IGSC 2024</a>]</span>."},{"p":"The weakness of that machinery has always been clear: it is self-regulation and covers only providers that joined. The 2024 US framework for nucleic-acid synthesis screening used an indirect but effective lever — not direct regulation but funding conditions: federally funded research may order only from providers that attest to compliance <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ostp2024\" data-ref=\"ostp2024\">OSTP 2024</a>]</span>. Using the market to make screening the default is much faster than legislating it. A voluntary commitment by more than 170 research-group leaders in 2024, David Baker among them, is another layer: report concerning practices and buy synthetic DNA only from providers that screen <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-responsiblebiodesign2024\" data-ref=\"responsiblebiodesign2024\">Responsible AI x Biodesign 2024</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Can design tools get past screening software? One assessment that was done right"},{"p":"In 2025 one study asked that question directly, and its <b>procedure</b> is more instructive than its conclusion <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wittmann2025\" data-ref=\"wittmann2025\">Wittmann 2025</a>]</span>:"},{"steps":["It was run entirely in silico, with <b>nothing synthesised</b>.","When gaps in recognition were found they were not published straight away but handled by <b>coordinated disclosure</b>, the practice of software security: notify the software developers and synthesis providers first, patch, then publish.","The sensitive sequences were kept out of the public record. What was published is that a gap existed, that it was closed and how the process worked — not how to reproduce it."]},{"p":"The same year the US National Institute of Standards and Technology ran an experimental assessment using <b>harmless stand-in proteins</b>: the tools of the day could not reliably both escape recognition and keep function, and the conclusion was honest — the gap is mitigated, but “its true severity remains unclear” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ikonomova2025\" data-ref=\"ikonomova2025\">Ikonomova 2025</a>]</span>. Read together the two are a good template: one purely computational, one experimental with harmless stand-ins, neither touching anything genuinely dangerous, and both patching before publishing."},{"h":"Why layers: a little arithmetic"},{"demo":"layers"},{"p":"The demo computes just two things. For <b>independent</b> gates the miss rate is a product: three gates at 90 % sensitivity miss <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.</mn><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. For <b>fully correlated</b> gates — all leaning on the same database, the same alignment method, the same threshold — the overall sensitivity collapses back to the best single gate and the miss rate is 10 %, a hundredfold worse."},{"p":"That factor of a hundred is the entire argument for making the gates independent of each other, and it is the most practical piece of governance advice: customer checks, sequence comparison, human review and post-use monitoring each rest on different information, so their failures are uncorrelated. The arithmetic is an old friend in chemistry: layer-of-protection analysis in <a href=\"../chemistry/chemeng/\">process safety</a> uses the same expression, and common-cause failure — everything going down in one power cut — is its greatest enemy."},{"h":"The part that is not solved"},{"list":["<b>Global unevenness.</b> A peer-reviewed governance analysis in 2026 pointed out that screening capacity differs sharply between countries while orders cross borders, so unilateral strictness is easy to route around <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zakaria2026\" data-ref=\"zakaria2026\">Zakaria 2026</a>]</span>. That is a policy problem, not a technical one.","<b>Every generation of models needs it redone.</b> The 2025 assessment was of the tools of that moment. When the models change generation, the question of whether screening still recognises their output has to be asked again, and the patch cycle is slower than the model cycle.","<b>Withholding weights is both risk control and an obstacle to scrutiny.</b> AlphaProteo and Chai-2 release no weights, biosecurity among the reasons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a>; <a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a>]</span>; the cost is that nobody outside can check their success rates independently (<a href=\"#c-wet\" data-open=\"wet\">10 · How to read a design result: evidence, denominators and error bars</a>). That is a genuine dilemma, not a question of who is right.","<b>What has to be screened is changing.</b> Today’s list-based regime compares sequence similarity, while a designed protein can resemble nothing on a list in sequence and still be functionally related. How to screen by <b>function</b> rather than by sequence similarity is the central open difficulty in the public discussion."]},{"p":"A last word on attitude. The previous ten chapters list dozens of results, and we tried to label the grade of evidence on each, because <b>overstating capability and understating risk are two sides of one error</b>. If a report’s hit rate cannot be checked, then judgements of how dangerous the work is, built on that report, are equally unreliable. Reading the numbers accurately is the precondition for having the safety discussion rest on facts."}],"points":[{"h":"The gate is at synthesis, not at design","p":"Designing a sequence needs no permission; turning it into a molecule almost always means ordering from a provider, which is where screening sits <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-igsc2024\" data-ref=\"igsc2024\">IGSC 2024</a>]</span>.","st":"history"},{"h":"Independent gates multiply; correlated gates do not","p":"Three gates at 90 %: a 0.1 % miss rate when independent and 10 % when fully correlated. The factor of a hundred is the rigorous basis for layered defence.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Coordinated disclosure works in this field","p":"The 2025 assessment notified developers and providers, patched, then published, keeping sensitive sequences out of the public record <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wittmann2025\" data-ref=\"wittmann2025\">Wittmann 2025</a>]</span>. It is a repeatable procedure.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Experimental assessment with harmless stand-ins is feasible","p":"The NIST work reached its conclusion with stand-in proteins and said plainly that “its true severity remains unclear” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ikonomova2025\" data-ref=\"ikonomova2025\">Ikonomova 2025</a>]</span>.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Funding conditions are a faster lever than legislation","p":"Federally funded research may order only from providers that attest to compliance <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ostp2024\" data-ref=\"ostp2024\">OSTP 2024</a>]</span>, using the market to make screening the default.","st":"history"},{"h":"Screening by function rather than sequence similarity is unsolved","p":"A designed protein can resemble nothing on a list and still be functionally related, which is the central open difficulty in the public discussion.","st":"open"},{"h":"Global screening capacity is uneven","p":"Orders cross borders, so unilateral strictness is easy to route around <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zakaria2026\" data-ref=\"zakaria2026\">Zakaria 2026</a>]</span>. This is a policy problem.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>miss</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>all</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Layered defence with independent gates: chain gates of sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and the miss rate is a product. Three at 90 % give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>fully correlated:</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>all</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The limit of common-cause failure: when every gate rests on the same source of information, adding gates buys nothing."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>all</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Reality lies between the two limits, set by the correlation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> between gates. The demo makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> a slider."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>missed/year</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>orders</mtext></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>⋅</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Converting a miss rate into an absolute count: the larger the order volume, the more absolute failures the same miss rate implies, so sensitivity has to rise with scale."}],"example":{"q":"A screening system has three gates with sensitivities of 95 %, 90 % and 80 %. What is the overall sensitivity if they are independent? And if all three rest on the same sequence database?","steps":["Independent: the miss rate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.10</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.20</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","Overall sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.001</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>99.9</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","Fully correlated: the overall sensitivity collapses to the best single gate, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the miss rate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","A factor of 50 between them. In absolute terms: with a million synthesis orders a year of which one in ten thousand merits attention (100 orders), independence misses 0.1 of them and full correlation misses 5."],"a":"Independent gives 99.9 % and fully correlated only 95 % — a fiftyfold difference in the miss rate. The arithmetic yields a concrete piece of governance advice: <b>adding a gate that rests on the same information as the existing ones buys almost no safety</b>; to buy any, the new gate has to rest on different information — customer identity, usage records, function rather than sequence similarity, downstream monitoring. It is also why screening in practice is never a single sequence comparison. Note that the conclusion does not depend on the particular numbers: once gates are correlated the multiplication fails, which is a property of probability."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-igsc2024\" data-ref=\"igsc2024\">IGSC 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ostp2024\" data-ref=\"ostp2024\">OSTP 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-responsiblebiodesign2024\" data-ref=\"responsiblebiodesign2024\">Responsible AI x Biodesign 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zambaldi2024\" data-ref=\"zambaldi2024\">Zambaldi 2024</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chai2025\" data-ref=\"chai2025\">Chai-2 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ikonomova2025\" data-ref=\"ikonomova2025\">Ikonomova 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wittmann2025\" data-ref=\"wittmann2025\">Wittmann 2025</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zakaria2026\" data-ref=\"zakaria2026\">Zakaria 2026</a>"}}};
